window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-12-2", "source": "2020年12月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay on why students should be encouraged to effective communication skills. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "Effective communication is far more than a soft skill; it is a core competence that shapes a student's academic\nsuccess and professional future. For this reason, schools and teachers should actively encourage students to\ndevelop this valuable ability from an early stage.\nThere are several compelling reasons for this. First, strong communication skills facilitate learning. When\nstudents can clearly express doubts and exchange ideas in group projects, knowledge is absorbed far more\nefficiently. Moreover, these skills sharpen employability. In job interviews, an applicant who articulates\nthoughts logically leaves a stronger impression than one who merely lists qualifications. In addition, good\ncommunication enriches personal relationships, helping students voice their emotions and resolve conflicts\nwith empathy. Such emotional connection is essential for a healthy campus life.\nIn conclusion, effective communication benefits students in study, career and daily life alike. Schools should\ntherefore create more opportunities—debates, presentations and teamwork—so that every student can grow\ninto a confident and articulate communicator."}, "translation": {"prompt": "港珠澳大桥(Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau Bridge)全长55公里，是我国一项不同寻常的工程壮举。大桥将三个城市连接起来，是世界上最长的跨海桥梁和隧道系统。大桥将三个城市之间的旅行时间从3小时缩短到30分钟。这座跨度巨大的钢筋混凝土大桥充分证明中国有能力建造创纪录的巨型建筑。它将助推区域一体化，促进经济增长。大桥是中国发展自己的大湾区总体规划的关键。中国希望将大湾区建成在技术创新和经济繁荣上能与旧金山、纽约和东京的湾区相媲美的地区。", "sample": "The Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau Bridge, with a total length of 55 kilometers, is an extraordinary engineering feat in China. It is the longest sea-crossing bridge-and-tunnel system in the world, connecting the three cities. It cuts the travel time between the three cities from three hours to 30 minutes. This massive reinforced concrete bridge fully proves that China is capable of building record-breaking mega- structures. It will boost regional integration and promote economic growth. The bridge is key to China’s master plan to develop its own Greater Bay Area. China hopes to turn the Greater Bay Area into a region that rivals the bay areas of San Francisco, New York and Tokyo in technological innovation and economic prosperity."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2020-12-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Virtually every activity that entails or facilitates in-person human interaction seems to be in the midst of a total meltdown as the coronavirus(冠状病毒) outbreak erases Americans' desire to travel. Amtrak says bookings are down 50 percent and cancelations are up 300 percent. Hotels in San Francisco are experiencing  __26__  rates between 70 and 80 percent. Broadway goes dark on Thursday night. Universities, now emptying their campuses, have never tried online learning on this  __27__  . White-collar companies like Amazon, Apple, and the New York Times are asking employees to work from home for the  __28__  future.\nBut what happens after the coronavirus?\nIn some ways, the answer is: All the old normal stuff. The pandemic(大流行病) will take lives,  __29__  economies and destroy routines, but it will pass. Americans will never stop going to basketball games. They won't stop going on vacation. They'll meet to do business. No decentralizing technology so far—not telephones, not television, and not the internet—has dented that human desire to shake hands, despite technologists'  __30__  to the contrary.\nYet there are real reasons to think that things will not return to the way they were last week. Small  __31__  create small societal shifts; big ones change things for good. The New York transit strike of 1980 is  __32__  with prompting several long-term changes in the city, including bus and bike lanes, and women wearing sports shoes to work. The Spanish flu pandemic of 1918 prompted the development of national health care in Europe.\nHere and now, this might not even be a question of  __33__  . It's not clear that the cruise industry will  __34__  . Or that public transit won't go broke without  __35__  assistance. The infrastructure might not even be in place to do what we were doing in 2019.", "matchIntro": "Slow Hope", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Our world is full of—mostly untold—stories of slow hope, driven by the idea that change is possible. They are 'slow' in their unfolding, and they are slow because they come with setbacks."}, {"label": "B", "text": "At the beginning of time—so goes the myth—humans suffered, shivering in the cold and dark until the titan(巨人) Prometheus stole fire from the gods. Just as in the myth, technology—first fire and stone tools, and later farming, the steam engine and industry, fossil fuels, chemicals and nuclear power—has allowed us to alter and control the natural world. The myth also reminds us that these advances have come at a price: as a punishment for Prometheus' crime, the gods created Pandora, and they gave her a box filled with evils and curses. When Pandora's box was opened, it unleashed swarms of diseases and disasters upon humankind."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Today we can no longer ignore the ecological curses that we have released in our search for warmth and comfort. In engineering and exploiting and transforming our habitat, we have opened tens of thousands of Pandora's boxes. In recent decades, environmental threats have expanded beyond regional boundaries to have global reach and, most hauntingly, are multiplying at a dizzying rate. On a regular basis, we are reminded that we are running out of time. Year after year, faster and faster, consumption outpaces the biological capacity of our planet. Stories of accelerated catastrophe multiply. We fear the breakdown of the electric grid, the end of non-renewable resources, the expansion of deserts, the loss of islands, and the pollution of our air and water."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acceleration is the signature of our time. Populations and economic activity grew slowly for much of human history. For thousands of years and well into early modern times, world economies saw no growth at all, but from around the mid-19th century and again, in particular, since the mid-20th, the real GDP has increased at an enormous speed, and so has human consumption. In the Middle Ages, households in Central Europe might have owned fewer than 30 objects on average; in 1900, this number had increased to 400, and in 2020 to 15,000. The acceleration of human production, consumption and travel has changed the animate and inanimate spheres. It has echoed through natural processes on which humans depend. Species extinction, deforestation, damming of rivers, occurrence of floods, the depletion of ozone, the degradation of ocean systems and many other areas are all experiencing acceleration. If represented graphically, the curve for all these changes looks rather like that well-known hockey stick: with little change over millennia(数千年) and a dramatic upswing over the past decades."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Some of today's narratives about the future seem to suggest that we too, like Prometheus, will be saved by a new Hercules, a divine engineer, someone who will mastermind, manoeuvre and manipulate our planet. They suggest that geoengineering, cold fusion or faster-than-light spaceships might transcend once and for all the terrestrial constraints of rising temperatures, lack of energy, scarcity of food, lack of space, mountains of waste, polluted water—you name it."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Yet, if we envisage our salvation to come from a deus ex machina(解围之神), from a divine engineer or a tech solutionist who will miraculously conjure up a new source of energy or another cure-all with revolutionary potency, we might be looking in the wrong place. The fact that we now imagine our planet as a whole does not mean that the 'rescue' of our planet will come with one big global stroke of genius and technology. It will more likely come by many small acts. Global heating and environmental degradation are not technological problems. They are highly political issues that are informed by powerful interests. Moreover, if history is a guide, then we can assume that any major transformations will once again be followed by a huge set of unintended consequences. So what do we do?"}, {"label": "G", "text": "This much is clear: we need to find ways that help us flatten the hockey-stick curves that reflect our ever-faster pace of ecological destruction and social acceleration. If we acknowledge that human manipulation of the Earth has been a destructive force, we can also imagine that human endeavours can help us build a less destructive world in the centuries to come. We might keep making mistakes. But we will also keep learning from our mistakes."}, {"label": "H", "text": "To counter the fears of disaster, we need to identify stories, visions and actions that work quietly towards a more hopeful future. Instead of one big narrative, a story of unexpected rescue by a larger-than-life hero, we need multiple stories: we need stories, not only of what Rob Nixon of Princeton University has called the 'slow violence' of environmental degradation (that is, the damage that is often invisible at first and develops slowly and gradually), but also stories of what I call 'slow hope'."}, {"label": "I", "text": "We need an acknowledgement of our present ecological plight but also a language of positive change, visions of a better future. In The Principle of Hope (1954-1959), Ernst Bloch, one of the leading philosophers of the future, wrote that 'the most tragic form of loss…is the loss of the capacity to imagine that things could be different'. We need to identify visions and paths that will help us imagine a different, more just and more ecological world. Hope, for Bloch, has its starting point in fear, in uncertainty, and in crisis: it is a creative force that goes hand in hand with utopian(乌托邦的) 'wishful images'. It can be found in cultural products of the past—in fairy tales, in fiction, in architecture, in music, in the movies—in products of the human mind that contain 'the outlines of a better world'. What makes us 'authentic' as humans are visions of our 'potential'. In other words: living in hope makes us human."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The power of small, grassroots movements to make changes that spread beyond their place of origin can be seen with the Slow Food movement, which began in Italy in the 1980s. The rise of fast-food restaurants after the Second World War produced a society full of cheap, industrially made foodstuffs. Under the leadership of Carlo Petrini, the Slow Food movement began in Piedmont, a region of Italy with a long history of poverty, violence and resistance to oppression. The movement transformed it into a region hospitable to traditional food cultures—based on native plants and breeds of animals. Today, Slow Food operates in more than 160 countries, poor and rich. It has given rise to thousands of projects around the globe, representing democratic politics, food sovereignty, biodiversity and sustainable agriculture."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The unscrupulous(无所顾忌的) commodification of food and the destruction of foodstuffs will continue to devastate soils, livelihoods and ecologies. Slow Food cannot undo the irresistible developments of the global food economy, but it can upset its theorists, it can 'speak differently', and it can allow people and their local food traditions and environments to flourish. Even in the United States—the fast-food nation—small farms and urban gardens are on the rise. The US Department of Agriculture provides an Urban Agriculture Toolkit and, according to a recent report, American millennials(千禧一代) are changing their diets. In 2017, 6 per cent of US consumers claimed to be strictly vegetarian, up from 1 per cent in 2014. As more people realise that 'eating is an agricultural act', as the US poet and environmental activist Wendell Berry put it in 1989, slow hope advances."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion(强迫) is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet.\" Nowhere is this more visible than in India.\nEarlier this year, the EAT-Lancet Commission released its global report on nutrition and called for a global shift to a more plant-based diet and for \"substantially reducing consumption of animal source foods.\" In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.\nThe EAT report presumes that \"traditional diets\" in countries like India include little red meat, which might be consumed only on special occasions or as minor ingredients in mixed dishes.\nIn India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc. Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy \"vegetarian diet\" on a meat-eating population as a way of providing the cheapest sources of food.\nCurrently, under an aggressive Hindu nationalist government, Muslims, Christians, disadvantaged classes and indigenous communities are being compelled to give up their traditional foods.\nNone of these concerns seem to have been appreciated by the EAT-Lancet Commission's representative, Brent Loken, who said \"India has got such a great example\" in sourcing protein from plants.\nBut how much of a model for the world is India's vegetarianism? In the Global Hunger Index 2019, the country ranks 102nd out of 117. Data from the National Family Health Survey indicate that only 10 percent of infants of 6 to 23 months are adequately fed.\nWhich is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.\nA diet directed at the affluent West fails to recognize that in low-income countries undernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal source foods, improving cognitive functions, while reducing the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies as well as mortality.\nEAT-Lancet claimed its intention was to \"spark conversations\" among all Indian stakeholders. Yet vocal critics of the food processing industry and food fortification strategies have been left out of the debate. But the most conspicuous omission may well be the absence of India's farmers.\nThe government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a model full of danger for future generations.", "47": "Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion(强迫) is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet.\" Nowhere is this more visible than in India.\nEarlier this year, the EAT-Lancet Commission released its global report on nutrition and called for a global shift to a more plant-based diet and for \"substantially reducing consumption of animal source foods.\" In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.\nThe EAT report presumes that \"traditional diets\" in countries like India include little red meat, which might be consumed only on special occasions or as minor ingredients in mixed dishes.\nIn India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc. Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy \"vegetarian diet\" on a meat-eating population as a way of providing the cheapest sources of food.\nCurrently, under an aggressive Hindu nationalist government, Muslims, Christians, disadvantaged classes and indigenous communities are being compelled to give up their traditional foods.\nNone of these concerns seem to have been appreciated by the EAT-Lancet Commission's representative, Brent Loken, who said \"India has got such a great example\" in sourcing protein from plants.\nBut how much of a model for the world is India's vegetarianism? In the Global Hunger Index 2019, the country ranks 102nd out of 117. Data from the National Family Health Survey indicate that only 10 percent of infants of 6 to 23 months are adequately fed.\nWhich is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.\nA diet directed at the affluent West fails to recognize that in low-income countries undernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal source foods, improving cognitive functions, while reducing the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies as well as mortality.\nEAT-Lancet claimed its intention was to \"spark conversations\" among all Indian stakeholders. Yet vocal critics of the food processing industry and food fortification strategies have been left out of the debate. But the most conspicuous omission may well be the absence of India's farmers.\nThe government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a model full of danger for future generations.", "48": "Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion(强迫) is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet.\" Nowhere is this more visible than in India.\nEarlier this year, the EAT-Lancet Commission released its global report on nutrition and called for a global shift to a more plant-based diet and for \"substantially reducing consumption of animal source foods.\" In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.\nThe EAT report presumes that \"traditional diets\" in countries like India include little red meat, which might be consumed only on special occasions or as minor ingredients in mixed dishes.\nIn India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc. Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy \"vegetarian diet\" on a meat-eating population as a way of providing the cheapest sources of food.\nCurrently, under an aggressive Hindu nationalist government, Muslims, Christians, disadvantaged classes and indigenous communities are being compelled to give up their traditional foods.\nNone of these concerns seem to have been appreciated by the EAT-Lancet Commission's representative, Brent Loken, who said \"India has got such a great example\" in sourcing protein from plants.\nBut how much of a model for the world is India's vegetarianism? In the Global Hunger Index 2019, the country ranks 102nd out of 117. Data from the National Family Health Survey indicate that only 10 percent of infants of 6 to 23 months are adequately fed.\nWhich is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.\nA diet directed at the affluent West fails to recognize that in low-income countries undernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal source foods, improving cognitive functions, while reducing the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies as well as mortality.\nEAT-Lancet claimed its intention was to \"spark conversations\" among all Indian stakeholders. Yet vocal critics of the food processing industry and food fortification strategies have been left out of the debate. But the most conspicuous omission may well be the absence of India's farmers.\nThe government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a model full of danger for future generations.", "49": "Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion(强迫) is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet.\" Nowhere is this more visible than in India.\nEarlier this year, the EAT-Lancet Commission released its global report on nutrition and called for a global shift to a more plant-based diet and for \"substantially reducing consumption of animal source foods.\" In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.\nThe EAT report presumes that \"traditional diets\" in countries like India include little red meat, which might be consumed only on special occasions or as minor ingredients in mixed dishes.\nIn India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc. Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy \"vegetarian diet\" on a meat-eating population as a way of providing the cheapest sources of food.\nCurrently, under an aggressive Hindu nationalist government, Muslims, Christians, disadvantaged classes and indigenous communities are being compelled to give up their traditional foods.\nNone of these concerns seem to have been appreciated by the EAT-Lancet Commission's representative, Brent Loken, who said \"India has got such a great example\" in sourcing protein from plants.\nBut how much of a model for the world is India's vegetarianism? In the Global Hunger Index 2019, the country ranks 102nd out of 117. Data from the National Family Health Survey indicate that only 10 percent of infants of 6 to 23 months are adequately fed.\nWhich is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.\nA diet directed at the affluent West fails to recognize that in low-income countries undernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal source foods, improving cognitive functions, while reducing the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies as well as mortality.\nEAT-Lancet claimed its intention was to \"spark conversations\" among all Indian stakeholders. Yet vocal critics of the food processing industry and food fortification strategies have been left out of the debate. But the most conspicuous omission may well be the absence of India's farmers.\nThe government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a model full of danger for future generations.", "50": "Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion(强迫) is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet.\" Nowhere is this more visible than in India.\nEarlier this year, the EAT-Lancet Commission released its global report on nutrition and called for a global shift to a more plant-based diet and for \"substantially reducing consumption of animal source foods.\" In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political situation and stress already undernourished populations.\nThe EAT report presumes that \"traditional diets\" in countries like India include little red meat, which might be consumed only on special occasions or as minor ingredients in mixed dishes.\nIn India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc. Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy \"vegetarian diet\" on a meat-eating population as a way of providing the cheapest sources of food.\nCurrently, under an aggressive Hindu nationalist government, Muslims, Christians, disadvantaged classes and indigenous communities are being compelled to give up their traditional foods.\nNone of these concerns seem to have been appreciated by the EAT-Lancet Commission's representative, Brent Loken, who said \"India has got such a great example\" in sourcing protein from plants.\nBut how much of a model for the world is India's vegetarianism? In the Global Hunger Index 2019, the country ranks 102nd out of 117. Data from the National Family Health Survey indicate that only 10 percent of infants of 6 to 23 months are adequately fed.\nWhich is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.\nA diet directed at the affluent West fails to recognize that in low-income countries undernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal source foods, improving cognitive functions, while reducing the prevalence of nutritional deficiencies as well as mortality.\nEAT-Lancet claimed its intention was to \"spark conversations\" among all Indian stakeholders. Yet vocal critics of the food processing industry and food fortification strategies have been left out of the debate. But the most conspicuous omission may well be the absence of India's farmers.\nThe government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a model full of danger for future generations.", "51": "Back in 1964, in his book Games People Play, psychiatrist Eric Berne described a pattern of conversation he called \"Why Don't You—Yes But\", which remains one of the most irritating aspects of everyday social life. The person adopting the strategy is usually a chronic complainer. Something is terrible about their relationship, job, or other situation, and they moan about it ceaselessly, but find some excuse to dismiss any solution that's proposed. The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can \"win\" the game—dismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor(对话者) gives up in annoyance—they get to feel pleasurably righteous(正当的) in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.\nPart of the trouble here is the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy(谬误). When you're feeling hard done by—taken for granted by your partner, say, or obliged to work for a half-witted boss—it's easy to become attached to the position that it's not your job to address the matter, and that doing so would be an admission of fault. But there's a confusion here. For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility. There would be choices to make, and no possibility of avoiding them, since trying to ignore the matter would be a choice. The point is that what goes for the baby on the doorstep is true in all cases: even if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from using this as a justification to evade responsibility.\nShould you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently. Psychotherapist Lori Gottlieb describes this as \"over-validation\". For one thing, you'll be spared further moaning, since the other person's motivation was to confirm her beliefs, and now you're confirming them. But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly seems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things.\n\"And then, sometimes, something magical might happen,\" Gottlieb writes. The other person \"might realise she's not as trapped as you are saying she is, or as she feels.\" Which illustrates the irony of the responsibility/fault fallacy: evading responsibility feels comfortable, but turns out to be a prison; whereas assuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing.", "52": "Back in 1964, in his book Games People Play, psychiatrist Eric Berne described a pattern of conversation he called \"Why Don't You—Yes But\", which remains one of the most irritating aspects of everyday social life. The person adopting the strategy is usually a chronic complainer. Something is terrible about their relationship, job, or other situation, and they moan about it ceaselessly, but find some excuse to dismiss any solution that's proposed. The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can \"win\" the game—dismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor(对话者) gives up in annoyance—they get to feel pleasurably righteous(正当的) in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.\nPart of the trouble here is the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy(谬误). When you're feeling hard done by—taken for granted by your partner, say, or obliged to work for a half-witted boss—it's easy to become attached to the position that it's not your job to address the matter, and that doing so would be an admission of fault. But there's a confusion here. For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility. There would be choices to make, and no possibility of avoiding them, since trying to ignore the matter would be a choice. The point is that what goes for the baby on the doorstep is true in all cases: even if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from using this as a justification to evade responsibility.\nShould you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently. Psychotherapist Lori Gottlieb describes this as \"over-validation\". For one thing, you'll be spared further moaning, since the other person's motivation was to confirm her beliefs, and now you're confirming them. But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly seems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things.\n\"And then, sometimes, something magical might happen,\" Gottlieb writes. The other person \"might realise she's not as trapped as you are saying she is, or as she feels.\" Which illustrates the irony of the responsibility/fault fallacy: evading responsibility feels comfortable, but turns out to be a prison; whereas assuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing.", "53": "Back in 1964, in his book Games People Play, psychiatrist Eric Berne described a pattern of conversation he called \"Why Don't You—Yes But\", which remains one of the most irritating aspects of everyday social life. The person adopting the strategy is usually a chronic complainer. Something is terrible about their relationship, job, or other situation, and they moan about it ceaselessly, but find some excuse to dismiss any solution that's proposed. The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can \"win\" the game—dismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor(对话者) gives up in annoyance—they get to feel pleasurably righteous(正当的) in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.\nPart of the trouble here is the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy(谬误). When you're feeling hard done by—taken for granted by your partner, say, or obliged to work for a half-witted boss—it's easy to become attached to the position that it's not your job to address the matter, and that doing so would be an admission of fault. But there's a confusion here. For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility. There would be choices to make, and no possibility of avoiding them, since trying to ignore the matter would be a choice. The point is that what goes for the baby on the doorstep is true in all cases: even if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from using this as a justification to evade responsibility.\nShould you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently. Psychotherapist Lori Gottlieb describes this as \"over-validation\". For one thing, you'll be spared further moaning, since the other person's motivation was to confirm her beliefs, and now you're confirming them. But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly seems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things.\n\"And then, sometimes, something magical might happen,\" Gottlieb writes. The other person \"might realise she's not as trapped as you are saying she is, or as she feels.\" Which illustrates the irony of the responsibility/fault fallacy: evading responsibility feels comfortable, but turns out to be a prison; whereas assuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing.", "54": "Back in 1964, in his book Games People Play, psychiatrist Eric Berne described a pattern of conversation he called \"Why Don't You—Yes But\", which remains one of the most irritating aspects of everyday social life. The person adopting the strategy is usually a chronic complainer. Something is terrible about their relationship, job, or other situation, and they moan about it ceaselessly, but find some excuse to dismiss any solution that's proposed. The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can \"win\" the game—dismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor(对话者) gives up in annoyance—they get to feel pleasurably righteous(正当的) in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.\nPart of the trouble here is the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy(谬误). When you're feeling hard done by—taken for granted by your partner, say, or obliged to work for a half-witted boss—it's easy to become attached to the position that it's not your job to address the matter, and that doing so would be an admission of fault. But there's a confusion here. For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility. There would be choices to make, and no possibility of avoiding them, since trying to ignore the matter would be a choice. The point is that what goes for the baby on the doorstep is true in all cases: even if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from using this as a justification to evade responsibility.\nShould you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently. Psychotherapist Lori Gottlieb describes this as \"over-validation\". For one thing, you'll be spared further moaning, since the other person's motivation was to confirm her beliefs, and now you're confirming them. But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly seems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things.\n\"And then, sometimes, something magical might happen,\" Gottlieb writes. The other person \"might realise she's not as trapped as you are saying she is, or as she feels.\" Which illustrates the irony of the responsibility/fault fallacy: evading responsibility feels comfortable, but turns out to be a prison; whereas assuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing.", "55": "Back in 1964, in his book Games People Play, psychiatrist Eric Berne described a pattern of conversation he called \"Why Don't You—Yes But\", which remains one of the most irritating aspects of everyday social life. The person adopting the strategy is usually a chronic complainer. Something is terrible about their relationship, job, or other situation, and they moan about it ceaselessly, but find some excuse to dismiss any solution that's proposed. The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be validated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can \"win\" the game—dismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor(对话者) gives up in annoyance—they get to feel pleasurably righteous(正当的) in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.\nPart of the trouble here is the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy(谬误). When you're feeling hard done by—taken for granted by your partner, say, or obliged to work for a half-witted boss—it's easy to become attached to the position that it's not your job to address the matter, and that doing so would be an admission of fault. But there's a confusion here. For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility. There would be choices to make, and no possibility of avoiding them, since trying to ignore the matter would be a choice. The point is that what goes for the baby on the doorstep is true in all cases: even if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from using this as a justification to evade responsibility.\nShould you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an ingenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently. Psychotherapist Lori Gottlieb describes this as \"over-validation\". For one thing, you'll be spared further moaning, since the other person's motivation was to confirm her beliefs, and now you're confirming them. But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their complaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly seems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things.\n\"And then, sometimes, something magical might happen,\" Gottlieb writes. The other person \"might realise she's not as trapped as you are saying she is, or as she feels.\" Which illustrates the irony of the responsibility/fault fallacy: evading responsibility feels comfortable, but turns out to be a prison; whereas assuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-12-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) A driving test.", "B) A video game.", "C) Traffic routes.", "D) Cargo logistics."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） A video game.\n【定位】W: This is unbelievable. Unlike any video game I've ever played before, it's so boring, yet so\nrelaxing at the same time. How did you hear about this driving simulator?\n女：这太不可思议了。和我以前玩过的任何电子游戏都不一样，它既无聊又让人放松。你是\n怎么知道这款驾驶模拟器的？\n【信号】开篇点题 + 全段反复出现。对话第一句女方就用 Unlike any video game I've ever played\nbefore 与 this driving simulator 点出核心物『电子游戏 / 驾驶模拟器』，之后每一段两人\n都围着这款游戏说（P2 介绍它、P3 评价它、P4 看它的直播、P5 由它联想行业），首尾贯\n穿正是主旨题的定位信号。\n【替换】this driving simulator ↔ A video game.\n上位词概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA driving test. 蹭了对话里反复出现的 driving 一词，还呼应了后面男方报名的 a safe\ndriving course，但全段主聊的是游戏，driving course / test 只是末尾延伸的一个点，不\n是『主要』在谈的内容。\n借⽤原⽂：a safe driving course\nC) 原词陷阱\nTraffic routes. 借了男方 The routes go through parts of the country 与女方 traffic on\nthe road 里的原词，但路线、交通只是游戏带来的细节感受之一，远不能概括整段主题。\n借⽤原⽂：The routes go through parts of the country\nD) 原词陷阱\nCargo logistics. 直接搬了女方 the logistics industry、cargo logistics 的原词，但物流业\n只是玩游戏后引发的一个联想话题，不是两人从头谈到尾的核心物。\n借⽤原⽂：cargo logistics", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the man say about the driving simulator?", "options": ["A) He found it instructive and realistic.", "B) He bought it when touring Europe.", "C) He was really drawn to its other versions.", "D) He introduced it to his brother last year."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He found it instructive and realistic.\n【定位】M: My brother introduced it to me last year. I was surprised to find how educational and\nrealistic it was.\n男：是我哥哥去年介绍给我的。我惊讶地发现它如此有教育意义、如此真实。\n【信号】被问者男方的直接应答。本题问男方的说法，答案就落在男方开口评价游戏的那句 I was\nsurprised to find how educational and realistic it was；surprised to find（惊讶地发\n现）带明显的个人评价语气，是男方对游戏定性的直接表态，正是细节题该锁定的应答句。\n【替换】how educational and realistic it was ↔ He found it instructive and realistic.\n近义词替换 educational=instructive\nI was surprised to find ↔ He found it instructive and realistic.\n动词 find 对应表态\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nHe bought it when touring Europe. 蹭了游戏名 It's called Euro Truck Simulator 里的\nEuro（欧洲），制造『他在欧洲旅游时买的』假象；但录音从没说他『购买』，只说是哥哥\n介绍的，touring Europe 纯属虚构。\n借⽤原⽂：It's called Euro Truck Simulator\nC) 偷换概念\nHe was really drawn to its other versions. 借了录音 they have other versions as well\n的原词，但男方紧接着说 I was really drawn to the scenery——他被吸引的是『风景\nscenery』，不是『其他版本 other versions』，选项把吸引对象偷换了。\n借⽤原⽂：I was really drawn to the scenery\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe introduced it to his brother last year. 搬了录音 My brother introduced it to me\nlast year 的全部原词，却把方向反转——录音是『哥哥介绍给他』，选项说成『他介绍给\n哥哥』，谁介绍给谁正好颠倒。\n借⽤原⽂：My brother introduced it to me last year", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman say she really appreciates?", "options": ["A) Traveling all over the country.", "B) Driving from one city to another.", "C) The details in the driving simulator.", "D) The key role of the logistics industry."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The details in the driving simulator.\n【定位】W: But I really appreciate all the details that go into the game.\n女：但我真的很欣赏这款游戏里所有用心做出来的细节。\n【信号】态度信号词 really appreciate。女方亲口说 I really appreciate all the details that go\ninto the game，really appreciate 是直白的『看重』信号，紧跟其后的 all the details 就\n是她真正欣赏的对象；这种态度词后紧接的宾语正是细节题的答案落点。\n【替换】all the details that go into the game ↔ The details in the driving simulator.\ndetails 原词 + game=simulator 同指\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTraveling all over the country. 蹭了男方 parts of the country you don't normally see\n里的 country，但那是男方谈被风景吸引时说的，且女方 appreciate 的是 details，不是\n『走遍全国』，对象与人都对不上。\n借⽤原⽂：parts of the country you don't normally see\nB) 原词陷阱\nDriving from one city to another. 几乎照抄女方 driving from one city to another 的原\n词，但女方说这句是在描述游戏内容『就是把货从 A 运到 B』，紧接着才说她真正欣赏的是\ndetails——这句是铺垫，不是 appreciate 的对象。\n借⽤原⽂：driving from one city to another\nD) 偷换概念\nThe key role of the logistics industry. 借了女方 a new appreciation for the logistics\nindustry 的原词，但那是她欣赏 details 后『额外』生出的一个感受，且 a new\nappreciation 是顺带提及，本题问的 really appreciate 直接指向 details。\n借⽤原⽂：a new appreciation for the logistics industry", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What outcome does the woman expect from the driving simulator?", "options": ["A) Clearer road signs.", "B) More people driving safely.", "C) Stricter traffic rules.", "D) More self-driving trucks on the road."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） More people driving safely.\n【定位】W: But if it results in more drivers looking both ways before entering an intersection, I'd say\nthat's a positive outcome.\n女：但如果它能让更多司机在进入路口前左右观望（更小心），我会说那是个积极的结果。\n【信号】假设 + 积极评价的期望句。女方在末段用 if it results in more drivers looking both\nways…I'd say that's a positive outcome，if…I'd say that's a positive outcome 是典型\n的『假设带来的好结果』语气，正是 expect 的对象所在；这种带 if / positive outcome 的\n句子是结果题的定位信号。\n【替换】more drivers looking both ways before entering an intersection ↔ More people driving safely.\n具体行为概括为安全驾驶\nthat's a positive outcome ↔ More people driving safely.\npositive outcome 印证这是她期待的结果\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nClearer road signs. 蹭了男方 obeyed all the road signs and traffic rules 里的 road\nsigns，但那是男方描述直播玩家守规矩时说的，女方期待的是『人更小心驾驶』，不是\n『路标更清晰』，人和内容都不对。\n借⽤原⽂：obeyed all the road signs and traffic rules\nC) 原词陷阱\nStricter traffic rules. 借了男方 obeyed all the road signs and traffic rules 里的 traffic\nrules，但录音从没说要『更严的交规』，更没出自女方的期望句，纯属拿原词改造出的无中\n生有。\n借⽤原⽂：obeyed all the road signs and traffic rules\nD) 答非所问\nMore self-driving trucks on the road. 直接搬了女方 self-driving trucks may soon be\navailable 的原词，但女方说自动驾驶卡车是在『分析行业前景』时提的客观可能，不是她\n对模拟器期待的 outcome；本题问的期待结果落在末段那句安全驾驶上。\n借⽤原⽂：self-driving trucks may soon be available", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say about his life of being self-employed?", "options": ["A) It isn't so enjoyable as he expected.", "B) It isn't so motivating as he believed.", "C) It doesn't enable him to earn as much money as he used to.", "D) It doesn't seem to offer as much freedom as he anticipated."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It doesn't seem to offer as much freedom as he anticipated.\n【定位】There are obvious benefits, though I don't seem to have the freedom I anticipated as I just\ndon't seem able to decline work offers.\n（自雇）有明显的好处，不过我似乎并没有我原先预期的那种自由，因为我好像就是没法拒\n绝送上门的工作。\n【信号】转折 though 后才是真意。前半 There are obvious benefits 是铺垫好处，though I\ndon't seem to have the freedom I anticipated 里的 though 一转，转折后点出的\"自由\n不如预期\"才是男士真正想说的落点，自由职业话题里这种\"先扬后抑\"很典型。\n【替换】I don't seem to have the freedom I anticipated ↔\nIt doesn't seem to offer as much freedom as he anticipated\n第一人称原话→第三人称转述，核心词不变\n【排除】A) 未提及\n男士只说 There are obvious benefits（有明显好处），并未评价它\"不如预期那么\nenjoyable\"，It isn't so enjoyable as he expected. 是把\"有好处\"歪曲成\"不够享受\"，无中\n生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\n男士确实提到 money alone provides insufficient motivation（光靠钱动力不足），但这\n说的是\"钱\"不够激励，不是\"自雇本身不如他想的那么 motivating\"，It isn't so motivating\nas he believed. 借了 motivation 这个原词来骗\"听到就想选\"。\n借⽤原⽂：money alone provides insufficient motivation\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 money 只出现在 money alone provides insufficient motivation，谈的是\"动\n力\"不是\"收入多少\"；It doesn't enable him to earn as much money as he used to. 凭空\n编出\"赚得没以前多\"的比较，原文从未涉及收入升降。\n借⽤原⽂：money alone provides insufficient motivation", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say about managers?", "options": ["A) Not all of them care about their employees' behaviors.", "B) Few of them are aware of their employees' feelings.", "C) Few of them offer praise and reward to their employees.", "D) Not all of them know how to motivate their employees."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Not all of them know how to motivate their employees.\n【定位】Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care, of course. And certainly not all\nmanagers have a clue how to motivate people.\n当然，并非每位经理都那么有觉察、有关怀。而且，确实并非所有经理都懂得如何激励\n员工。\n【信号】范围词 not every／not all 直接定调。男士连用 Not every manager behaves with such\nawareness and care 和 not all managers have a clue how to motivate people，两\n个\"not every/not all\"是部分否定，明确说\"并非全部\"，这正是范围题的答案信号。\n【替换】not all managers have a clue how to motivate people ↔\nNot all of them know how to motivate their employees.\nhave a clue=know，people=employees，范围词原样保留\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音说 Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care，讲的是\"并非每位\n经理都有觉察与关怀\"，而非 Not all of them care about their employees' behaviors.\"不\n在意员工的行为\"——care 的对象被偷换：原文是经理自己有没有 care，选项变成\"在不在\n意员工的 behaviors\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 Few of them are aware of their employees' feelings. 用 Few（几乎没有）替换了\n原文的 not all（并非全部），范围被大幅夸大；且 awareness 原文修饰的是经理的觉察，\n不是专门\"察觉员工 feelings\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care\nC) 张冠李戴\nFew of them offer praise and reward to their employees. 里的 praise 来自女士那句\nThere's no one around to offer praise，谈的是\"自雇者身边没人给表扬\"，并非男士对\"经\n理群体\"的概括，且又把范围说成 Few，张冠李戴又改范围。\n借⽤原⽂：There's no one around to offer praise", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What do both speakers value most about self-employment?", "options": ["A) Job satisfaction.", "B) Self-awareness.", "C) Autonomy.", "D) Money."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Autonomy.\n【定位】The motivator I value most is autonomy. I've learned not to sacrifice my prized autonomy by\nworking all hours of the day and by saying yes to every client request.\n我最看重的动力来源是自主权。我已经学会不会因为整天连轴转、不会因为对客户的每个请\n求都说\"好\"，而牺牲掉我所珍视的自主权。\n【信号】最高级 value most 直接点名。女士说 The motivator I value most is autonomy，value\nmost 是最高级强调，直接把\"最看重的\"点成 autonomy；男士随后在第6段附和\"我选择这\n种生活方式正是为了 independence（独立）\"，双方一致，正合题干 both speakers。\n【替换】The motivator I value most is autonomy ↔ Autonomy.\n答案句直接念出原词 autonomy\n【排除】A) 未提及\n全篇没有任何一句说双方\"最看重 job satisfaction\"，Job satisfaction. 是听起来贴题却未被\n点名的合理项，属无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\nSelf-awareness. 借了第4段 Not every manager behaves with such awareness and\ncare 里的 awareness 一词，但那是说经理的觉察，跟\"两人最看重什么\"毫无关系。\n借⽤原⽂：Not every manager behaves with such awareness and care\nD) 与原文相反\nmoney 在录音里出现于 money alone provides insufficient motivation，恰恰是说\"光靠\n钱动力不够\"，与\"最看重 money\"相反，Money. 是典型听到原词就误选的陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：money alone provides insufficient motivation", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "On what point does the man agree with the woman?", "options": ["A) The importance of cultivating close relationships with clients.", "B) The need for getting recommendations from their managers.", "C) The advantages of permanent full-time employment.", "D) The way to explore employees' interests and talents."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The importance of cultivating close relationships with clients.\n【定位】You're right. That's an area I do need to put some more effort into—building closer\nrelationships with those who engage my services and skills.\n你说得对。这确实是我需要再多下点功夫的地方——和那些雇用我服务与技能的人建立更紧\n密的关系。\n【信号】表态词 You're right 后承接的观点。男士开口 You're right 明确表示赞同，紧接着复述他\n认同的那件事 building closer relationships with those who engage my services and\nskills，表态词后承接的内容就是\"他同意的那一点\"。\n【替换】building closer relationships with those who engage my services and skills ↔\nThe importance of cultivating close relationships with clients.\nthose who engage my services=clients，building=cultivating\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nThe need for getting recommendations from their managers. 里的\nrecommendations 来自女士第7段 getting written testimony and recommendations，\n但那是从客户处获取，选项偷改成\"从经理处获取\"，而自雇者根本没有经理，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：getting written testimony and recommendations\nC) 与原文相反\n录音里 permanent full-time employment 出现在男士 I don't miss aspects of\npermanent full-time employment I disliked，他说的是\"不怀念全职的那些方面\"，与\"全\n职的优点\"恰恰相反，The advantages of permanent full-time employment. 反了向。\n借⽤原⽂：I don't miss aspects of permanent full-time employment I disliked\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe way to explore employees' interests and talents. 里的 interests and talents 来自\n女士第3段 no one to make greater use of our interests and talents，谈的是自雇者没人\n来发掘自己的兴趣才能，并非男士\"赞同\"的那一点，张冠李戴又偏题。\n借⽤原⽂：no one to make greater use of our interests and talents", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about the findings of the new study?", "options": ["A) Consumers visualize their activities in different weather.", "B) Good weather triggers consumers' desire to go shopping.", "C) Weather conditions influence consumers' buying behavior.", "D) Consumers' mental states change with the prices of goods."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题） Weather conditions influence consumers' buying behavior.\n【定位】Now, a new study reveals how sunny and snowy conditions influence consumer behavior.\n如今，一项新研究揭示了晴天和雪天如何影响消费者行为。\n【信号】点题动词 reveals 引出发现。开头段用 a new study reveals how sunny and snowy\nconditions influence consumer behavior，reveals（揭示）是典型的科研报道点题信\n号，紧跟其后的就是研究的核心发现，这是主旨题的标准落点。\n【替换】sunny and snowy conditions influence consumer behavior ↔\nWeather conditions influence consumers' buying behavior\n上位词概括 + 同词换说\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 说消费者 \"想象不同天气下的活动\"，这只是后文解释机制时提到的 mentally visualize\nusing products 一个环节，不是研究的总发现，犯了以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：trigger consumers to mentally visualize using products\nB) 偷换概念\nB 把范围缩到 Good weather（好天气），又把落点改成 desire to go shopping（想去逛\n街）；录音讲的是 sunny and snowy（晴和雪）都起作用、影响的是 buying behavior\n（购买行为）而非逛街冲动，范围和落点都被偷改。\n借⽤原⽂：weather conditions trigger consumers\nD) 与原文相反\nD 说消费者心理 \"随商品价格变化\"，把因果调了头；录音是天气→心理→愿付更高价\n（placing a higher value on those products），价格是结果不是原因，方向正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：placing a higher value on those products", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the passage say may increase the value of products for consumers?", "options": ["A) Active consumption.", "B) Direct correlation.", "C) Individual association.", "D) Mental visualization."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Mental visualization.\n【定位】This mental picture of using the towel increases the value of the product in the\nconsumers' mind.\n这种使用毛巾的心理画面，提升了产品在消费者心中的价值。\n【信号】结果动词 increases the value 锁定。原文 This mental picture of using the towel\nincreases the value of the product 直接出现题干要找的 increases the value，其主语\nmental picture（心理画面）就是提升价值的原因，结果动词前的主语即答案。\n【替换】This mental picture of using the towel ↔ Mental visualization\n形象说法换术语\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 的 Active consumption（积极消费）录音从未出现，是临场看着 consumer 一词联想出\n来的干扰，与提升价值的机制无关。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 the correlation is only found with products related to being outside，B 盗\n用 correlation（相关性）这个原词；但 correlation 描述的是 \"天气与户外用品的关联范\n围\"，不是 \"提升价值的机制\"，听到原词就选会中招。\n借⽤原⽂：the correlation is only found with products\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第 4 段有 a positive association with outside activities，C 盗用 association（联想\n/ 关联）原词，又加上 Individual（个体的）；但原文说的是天气与户外活动的关联，不是\n\"个体联想\" 提升价值，词在、意思偏。\n借⽤原⽂：a positive association with outside activities", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "How can the findings of the new study benefit online sellers according to the researchers?", "options": ["A) Enabling them to simplify their mathematical formulas.", "B) Helping them determine what to sell and at what price.", "C) Enabling them to sell their products at a higher price.", "D) Helping them advertise a greater variety of products."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Helping them determine what to sell and at what price.\n【定位】Online sellers often use complex mathematical formulas to determine what products to\nfeature and how to price these products. Incorporating more data about weather would allow\nthem to make better decisions.\n在线卖家常用复杂的数学公式来决定主推哪些产品、如何定价。把更多天气数据纳入其中，\n能让他们做出更好的决策。\n【信号】对象词 + would allow them to 引出好处。录音点到对象 Online sellers 后，用\nIncorporating more data about weather would allow them to make better decisions\n给出好处；would allow them to（能让他们……）是 \"好处 / 用途\" 的信号词，紧随的就是\n答案内容。\n【替换】determine what products to feature and how to price these products ↔\ndetermine what to sell and at what price\n近义同构改写\nwould allow them to make better decisions ↔ Helping them determine\nmake better decisions = helping determine\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 盗用 mathematical formulas 原词，却说成 simplify their mathematical formulas\n（简化公式）；录音是卖家 use complex...formulas、再往里 incorporating more data\n（加进更多数据），是 \"加数据\" 不是 \"简化公式\"，方向相反。\n借⽤原⽂：use complex mathematical formulas\nC) 张冠李戴\nC 说让卖家 sell at a higher price（卖得更贵），这其实拿的是前文消费者 willing to pay\nmore money 的内容；本题问的是研究对卖家的好处——帮其决策，而非直接抬价，张冠\n李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：they're willing to pay more money for them\nD) 未提及\nD 说帮卖家 advertise a greater variety of products（推广更多种类产品），录音只在开\n头提 companies that sell a wide array of products（卖品类多的公司适用），但从未说\n研究帮他们 \"打广告\" 或 \"增加品类\"，属无中生有。\n借⽤原⽂：companies that sell a wide array of products online", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What is the accepted wisdom concerning the office environment?", "options": ["A) A naturally ventilated office is more comfortable.", "B) A cool office will boost employees' productivity.", "C) Office air-conditioning should follow guidebooks.", "D) Air-conditioning improves ventilation in the office."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） A cool office will boost employees' productivity.\n【定位】But recent studies have challenged the accepted wisdom that a cool office is more productive.\n但近期研究对一种公认看法提出了挑战，那就是认为凉爽的办公室更能提高生产力。\n【信号】accepted wisdom 后跟的同位语就是公认看法。信号是原文里 the accepted wisdom\nthat a cool office is more productive 中 accepted wisdom 这个词，它后面 that 引出的\n内容正是题目要的公认看法。虽然句子用 But challenged 在反驳它，但被反驳的对象本身\n才是 accepted wisdom 的内容。\n【替换】a cool office is more productive ↔ A cool office will boost employees' productivity\nmore productive 改写成 boost productivity\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA naturally ventilated office is more comfortable. 把自然通风和更舒适挂钩，但原文谈\n的是空调与生产力，并未说自然通风的办公室更舒适，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nOffice air-conditioning should follow guidebooks. 借用了原文 Numerous guidebooks\n里念到的 guidebooks 一词，听到 guidebooks 就想选；但原文只是说很多指南声称 22 度\n效率最高，并没有提出空调应当遵循指南这一主张，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Numerous guidebooks across the world on heating, ventilation and air-conditioning\nclaim office performance peaks at 22 degrees\nD) 偷换概念\nAir-conditioning improves ventilation in the office. 借了原文 ventilation 一词，但原文\nventilation 只是出现在 heating, ventilation and air-conditioning 这个并列短语里，从没\n说空调能改善通风，把不相干的两个词强行拼成因果，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：heating, ventilation and air-conditioning", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What did researchers find from their review of all studies relating to air-conditioning and productivity?", "options": ["A) People in their comfort zone of temperature are more satisfied with their productivity.", "B) People in different countries vary in their tolerance to uncomfortable temperatures.", "C) Twenty-two degrees is the optimal temperature for office workers.", "D) There is a range of temperatures for people to feel comfortable."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） There is a range of temperatures for people to feel comfortable.\n【定位】But there is a range of at least three to four degrees which does not have any adverse impact.\n但存在一个至少三到四度的温度范围，在这个范围内不会产生任何不良影响。\n【信号】but 转折后的 a range 是结论落点。信号是 But there is a range 里的 But。前面说人们总\n爱追求唯一的最佳温度，But 之后才是研究者要纠正的真意：存在一个温度范围。转折后的\n内容才是答案句。\n【替换】there is a range of at least three to four degrees ↔\nThere is a range of temperatures for people to feel comfortable\n区间表述对区间表述\nan optimal range would be between 23 and 26 degrees ↔ for people to feel comfortable\n舒适区改写成感到舒适\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nPeople in their comfort zone of temperature are more satisfied with their\nproductivity. 借用了原文的 comfort zone，但原文说舒适区的作用是不产生不良影响，从\n没说处在舒适区的人对自己的生产力更满意，satisfied with their productivity 是原文没有\n的说法，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：people can even tolerate temperatures beyond this comfort zone\nB) 未提及\nPeople in different countries vary in their tolerance to uncomfortable temperatures.\n提到不同国家的人耐受度不同，但原文谈的是温度范围与适应，全程没有提到国家之间的差\n异，属未提及。\nC) 与原文相反\nTwenty-two degrees is the optimal temperature for office workers. 借了原文反复念到\n的 22 度，听到 22 就想选；但原文研究者的发现恰恰是 22 degrees was probably a little\nchilly，22 度偏凉，最佳范围是 23 到 26 度，所以说 22 度是最佳值与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：22 degrees was probably a little chilly, even at the height of summer", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do we learn about using a little less air-conditioning during hot weather?", "options": ["A) It will have no negative impact on work.", "B) It will be immediately noticeable.", "C) It will sharply decrease work efficiency.", "D) It will cause a lot of discomfort."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It will have no negative impact on work.\n【定位】But there is a range of at least three to four degrees which does not have any adverse impact.\n但存在一个至少三到四度的温度范围，在这个范围内不会产生任何不良影响。\n【信号】does not have any adverse impact 是后果的否定信号。信号是原文的含蓄否定 does\nnot have any adverse impact（没有任何不良影响）。题干问热天少开空调（即把温度调\n高一点）会怎样，紧接其前的 it makes sense to turn the air-conditioning up 正是这个\n做法，其后果就是不产生不良影响。\n【替换】does not have any adverse impact ↔ It will have no negative impact on work\nadverse 等于 negative\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt will be immediately noticeable. 说会立刻被察觉，但原文只说没有不良影响，从没谈这\n个变化是否能立即被感知，属未提及。\nC) 与原文相反\nIt will sharply decrease work efficiency. 说会急剧降低工作效率，与原文 does not have\nany adverse impact（无不良影响）正好相反，把无害说成大害，方向反了。\n借⽤原⽂：which does not have any adverse impact\nD) 与原文相反\nIt will cause a lot of discomfort. 说会造成很多不适，但原文说人们能适应、且无不良影\n响，并未说热天少开空调会带来大量不适，方向与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：people can even tolerate temperatures beyond this comfort zone", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What happens when people are used to an air-conditioned environment?", "options": ["A) They tend to favor lower temperatures.", "B) They suffer from rapid temperature changes.", "C) They are not bothered by temperature extremes.", "D) They become less sensitive to high temperatures."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They tend to favor lower temperatures.\n【定位】If they're used to the environment which is air-conditioned, they tend to prefer\nlower temperatures.\n如果他们习惯了有空调的环境，就往往会更偏好较低的温度。\n【信号】If 条件句与题干 when 对应，主句即后果。信号是原文 If they're used to the\nenvironment which is air-conditioned 这个 If 条件，与题干 when 习惯空调环境完全对\n应，紧跟的主句 they tend to prefer lower temperatures 就是答案句。\n【替换】they tend to prefer lower temperatures ↔ They tend to favor lower temperatures\nprefer 等于 favor\n【排除】B) 未提及\nThey suffer from rapid temperature changes. 说他们受快速温度变化之苦，但原文只说\n他们偏好低温、并能逐渐适应新温度，从没提急剧温变带来痛苦，属未提及。\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey are not bothered by temperature extremes. 借用了原文 the extreme ends of the\ntemperature range 里的 extreme，听到就想选；但原文恰恰说在极端温度处人们的生产\n力会受损，并非不受困扰，与原文相反。\n借⽤原⽂：It was only at the extreme ends of the temperature range where people's\nproductivity suffered\nD) 偷换概念\nThey become less sensitive to high temperatures. 说他们对高温变得不敏感，原文虽提\n到人会适应新温度，但本题对应句说的是偏好更低温度，而非对高温迟钝，把适应偷换成对\n高温脱敏，方向也与偏好低温不符，属偷换概念。\n借⽤原⽂：almost all humans became accustomed to the new temperature", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is one of the criticisms directed at the early research on solitude?", "options": ["A) It overlooked the possibility that emotions may be controlled.", "B) It ignored the fact that emotions are personal and subjective.", "C) It classified emotions simply as either positive or negative.", "D) It measured positive and negative emotions independently."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It classified emotions simply as either positive or negative.\n【定位】Also, it categorized emotions as simply positive or negative.\n此外，它还把情绪简单地归为正面或负面两类。\n【信号】列举信号 Also 引出第二条缺陷。第二段先说旧研究 It had a couple of shortcomings（有\n几处不足），再用 First 和 Also 逐条列出；Also, it categorized emotions as simply\npositive or negative 正是第二条缺陷，列举词 Also 就是答案句的信号。\n【替换】categorized emotions as simply positive or negative ↔\nclassified emotions simply as either positive or negative\ncategorized 同义改写为 classified\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nemotions may be controlled 听着像录音里 people can use solitude to regulate their\nemotions（用独处调节情绪）的可控之意，但那是末段讲新研究的启示，不是早期研究的\n缺陷，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：people can use solitude to regulate their emotions\nB) 未提及\nemotions are personal and subjective 谈情绪的私人性与主观性，录音批评早期研究时只\n说它简单分类、未独立测量正负情绪，从头到尾没提主观、私人这一层，是无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\nmeasured positive and negative emotions independently 盗用了原文 our positive\nand negative emotions can fluctuate independently 里的 independently，但原文是说\n旧研究恰恰忽略了正负情绪可独立波动这一点，D 却说旧研究独立测量了，正好说反。\n借⽤原⽂：overlooking the possibility that our positive and negative emotions can\nfluctuate independently", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do we learn about the results of the new research?", "options": ["A) Sitting alone without doing anything seemed really distressing.", "B) Solitude adversely affected the participants' mental well-being.", "C) Sitting alone for 15 minutes made the participants restless.", "D) Solitude had a reductive effect on high-arousal emotions."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Solitude had a reductive effect on high-arousal emotions.\n【定位】This experiment specifically aimed to determine the effect of solitude on high-arousal\nemotions. ... After 15 minutes of solitude, the participants showed reductions in both types\nof emotion.\n这个实验专门用来测定独处对高唤醒情绪的影响。……独处十五分钟后，参与者的两类情绪\n都出现了下降。\n【信号】结果信号 The results were clear 后接 showed reductions。第三段先点明研究对象是\nhigh-arousal emotions（高唤醒情绪），随后用 The results were clear 拉出结果，\nshowed reductions in both types of emotion 即结论；results were clear 与 showed\n就是答案句信号。\n【替换】showed reductions in both types of emotion ↔ had a reductive effect on high-arousal emotions\nreductions 名词改成 reductive effect\nthe effect of solitude on high-arousal emotions ↔ high-arousal emotions\n实验对象原词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nreally distressing 盗用了原文 being scared or distressed 里的 distressed，但原文只是\n把 distressed 当作所测的一种负面情绪举例，并没说独坐这件事本身令人痛苦，属偷换概\n念。\n借⽤原⽂：negative emotions including being scared or distressed\nB) 张冠李戴\nadversely affected the participants' mental well-being 听着像末段 negative physical\nand psychological effects，但那是讲长期孤独 prolonged loneliness 的危害，而本研究\n测的是短暂独处、结果是削弱情绪，并非损害健康，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：the latter is correlated with an assortment of negative physical and psychological effects\nC) 未提及\nmade the participants restless 用了原文 the participants 这个词，但录音从没说参与者\n变得焦躁不安 restless，结果只是情绪下降；restless 是无中生有的负面反应。\n借⽤原⽂：They asked participants to spend 15 minutes sitting alone", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What did the second experiment in the new research find about solitude?", "options": ["A) It proved hard to depict objectively.", "B) It went hand in hand with sadness.", "C) It helped increase low-arousal emotions.", "D) It tended to intensify negative emotions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It helped increase low-arousal emotions.\n【定位】A second study measured the effects of solitude on low-arousal emotions. ... That experiment\nfound that all of these emotions were increased by time alone.\n第二项研究测定独处对低唤醒情绪的影响。……该实验发现，这些情绪都因独处时间而增\n强了。\n【信号】实验切换信号 A second study 引出新段结论。第四段以 A second study 切到第二个实\n验，对象是 low-arousal emotions（低唤醒情绪），随后 found that all of these\nemotions were increased by time alone 给出发现；A second study 与 found 就是答案\n句信号。\n【替换】all of these emotions were increased by time alone ↔ It helped increase low-arousal emotions\nwere increased 被动改成 helped increase 主动\nthe effects of solitude on low-arousal emotions ↔ low-arousal emotions\n实验对象原词对应\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nproved hard to depict objectively 谈低唤醒情绪难以客观刻画，原文虽提过 past\ndepictions of solitude were wrong 含 depict 一词，但说的是过去对独处的描述有误，不\n是说情绪难以客观描述，属偷换概念加未提及。\n借⽤原⽂：past depictions of solitude were wrong\nB) 原词陷阱\nwent hand in hand with sadness 盗用了原文 feeling calm, relaxed, sad or lonely 里的\nsad，但原文只是把 sad 列为所测低唤醒情绪之一，并没说独处与悲伤相伴，属以偏概全。\n借⽤原⽂：such as feeling calm, relaxed, sad or lonely\nD) 偷换概念\ntended to intensify negative emotions 中 intensify 对应原文 It amplifies quieter\nemotions 的增强义，但原文增强的是 quieter / low-arousal emotions（低唤醒情绪，含\n正负两类），D 却把对象缩成 negative emotions，偷换了增强的对象。\n借⽤原⽂：It amplifies quieter emotions, but it diminishes the intensity of stronger feelings", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn about the brain when the body is at rest?", "options": ["A) It uses up much less energy than it does in deep thinking.", "B) It remains inactive without burning calories noticeably.", "C) It continues to burn up calories to help us stay in shape.", "D) It consumes almost a quarter of the body's total energy."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It consumes almost a quarter of the body's total energy.\n【定位】Well, when the body is at rest, we know that the brain uses up a startling 20% to 25% of the\nbody's overall energy.\n嗯，当身体处于静止状态时，我们知道大脑会消耗掉身体总能量中惊人的 20% 到 25%。\n【信号】条件状语 + 数字落点。录音以 when the body is at rest 这个条件状语开头，与题干前提\n逐字对上，紧接着的数字 20% to 25% 就是答案落点——条件词一出现、数字一念出，这\n句即答案句。\n【替换】20% to 25% of the body's overall energy ↔ almost a quarter of the body's total energy\n比例换算成 a quarter\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 拿录音后文出现过的「深度思考」做文章，说静止时耗能远少于深度思考时；但答案句问\n的是静止时的具体比例，录音并未在此处做这种「静止 vs 深思」的耗能对比，属听到 deep\nthinking 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：deep thinking will not burn off the calories\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说大脑静止时「不活跃、几乎不烧卡路里」，但答案句明说静止时大脑就消耗高达 20%\nto 25% 的总能量，是「最耗能的器官」，与 B 的「inactive、不显著烧卡」正好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：the brain uses up a startling 20% to 25% of the body's overall energy\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 借用录音里的 burn up calories，说大脑持续烧卡路里「帮我们保持身材」；但「帮保持\n身材/减肥」是录音用反问引出后又否定的说法（deep thinking will not burn off the\ncalories），并非静止时大脑的事实，属盗用原词又错配结论。\n借⽤原⽂：Elite players can reportedly burn up to an absurd 6,000 calories in one day", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the consumption of the brain's energy?", "options": ["A) Much of the consumption has nothing to do with conscious activities.", "B) It has something to do with the difficulty of the activities in question.", "C) Energy usage devoted to active learning accounts for a big part of it.", "D) A significant amount of it is for performing difficult cognitive tasks."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Much of the consumption has nothing to do with conscious activities.\n【定位】We're unconscious of most of what uses up the brain's energy. A lot of that activity is\nunrelated to conscious activities like learning how to sing or play the guitar.\n我们对消耗大脑能量的大部分活动是无意识的。其中很多活动与有意识的活动（比如学唱歌\n或弹吉他）无关。\n【信号】占比措辞 most of / a lot of + 关联否定。录音用 most of what uses up the brain's\nenergy 点出消耗的「大部分」，再用 unrelated to conscious activities 给这大部分定性\n——「占比词 + 关联否定」两个信号叠在一句，正是给能量去向定性的答案句。\n【替换】A lot of that activity is unrelated to conscious activities ↔\nMuch of the consumption has nothing to do with conscious activities\nunrelated to = has nothing to do with\nmost of what uses up the brain's energy ↔ Much of the consumption\nmost of ↔ Much of 概括对应\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nB 说耗能「取决于所涉活动的难度」，借的是录音 cognitively difficult tasks 的难度概念；\n但本题问的是大脑总能量消耗的去向，录音明说难任务带来的额外耗能其实「相对极小」\n（comparatively tiny），并非耗能整体取决于难度，属答非所问。\n借⽤原⽂：Technically, the answer is yes, for cognitively difficult tasks\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说「主动学习的耗能占很大一部分」，盗用了录音里的学习例子；但答案句恰恰把唱歌弹\n吉他这类有意识学习划为「无关、且占小头」（unrelated to conscious activities），C 把\n占比方向说反了。\n借⽤原⽂：learning how to sing or play the guitar\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说「相当一部分能量用于完成困难的认知任务」，搬来录音的 difficult cognitive tasks；\n但录音说为多想一点而额外花的能量「相对极小」（comparatively tiny），并非占相当一\n部分，是盗用原词后把占比夸大。\n借⽤原⽂：the energy required just to think harder is actually comparatively tiny", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do we learn about the overall energy availability in the brain?", "options": ["A) It is believed to remain basically constant.", "B) It is a prerequisite for any mental activity.", "C) It is conducive to relieving mental exhaustion.", "D) It is thought to be related to food consumption."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is believed to remain basically constant.\n【定位】But the overall energy availability in the brain is thought to be constant.\n但大脑整体的能量供应被认为是恒定的。\n【信号】转折 But + 术语原词。录音先说大脑能给活跃区域调配血液能量，再用转折 But 一转，正\n面给出 the overall energy availability in the brain is thought to be constant——转折\n后的这句与题干术语逐字对上，是答案句；转折信号提示真意在后半句。\n【替换】the overall energy availability in the brain is thought to be constant ↔\nIt is believed to remain basically constant\nthought to be = believed to remain\n【排除】B) 过度推断\nB 说它是「任何心理活动的前提」，录音并未把整体能量供应说成 any mental activity 的\n先决条件，only 提到能量在区域间调配；B 用 any 拔高成绝对判断，属无中生有的推断。\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说它「有助于缓解精神疲劳」，借了录音末段的 mental exhaustion；但录音只是说棋手\n消瘦主要源于压力和进食减少、而非精神耗竭，从未说总能量供应能「缓解疲劳」，属盗用\n原词又凭空安结论。\n借⽤原⽂：it mostly comes down to stress and reduced food consumption, not mental exhaustion\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说它「与食物摄入有关」，盗用录音末段的 food consumption；但 reduced food\nconsumption 是录音用来解释棋手为何消瘦的，与「大脑整体能量供应恒定」这一判定无\n关，属把别处的词安到本题对象上。\n借⽤原⽂：reduced food consumption", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the job interview question \"tell me about yourself\"?", "options": ["A) Job candidates rarely take it seriously.", "B) Job seekers tend to have a ready answer.", "C) Job seekers often feel at a loss where to start in answering it.", "D) Job candidates can respond freely due to its open-ended nature."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Job seekers often feel at a loss where to start in answering it.\n【定位】\"Tell me about yourself\" may seem like an easy job interview question, but the open-ended\nnature of this question often leaves job seekers at a loss where to start.\n\"谈谈你自己\"看上去像个简单的面试问题，但这个问题的开放性常常让求职者不知道该从何\n说起。\n【信号】转折 but 之后才是真意。句子前半 may seem like an easy job interview question 是铺\n垫，真正态度在转折词 but 之后：the open-ended nature of this question often leaves\njob seekers at a loss where to start。听到 but 就知道前半是陪衬、后半才是落点。\n【替换】leaves job seekers at a loss where to start ↔\nJob seekers often feel at a loss where to start in answering it\n近乎原句的同义改写\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项称 Job candidates rarely take it seriously，但原文恰恰说这是\"a critical test\"、要认\n真准备，根本没有\"不当回事\"的意思，属凭空捏造。\nB) 与原文相反\n选项说 Job seekers tend to have a ready answer（有现成答案），正好与原文\"often\nleaves job seekers at a loss\"（常常无从下手）相反，是把说话人的判断反了过来。\n借⽤原⽂：often leaves job seekers at a loss where to start\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Job candidates can respond freely due to its open-ended nature 盗用了原文原词\nthe open-ended nature，但原文说\"开放性\"带来的是\"无从下手\"，不是\"可自由作答\"，听\n到 open-ended 就选正中陷阱。\n借⽤原⽂：the open-ended nature of this question often leaves job seekers at a loss", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What will most people do when they come to a job interview?", "options": ["A) Follow their career coaches' guidelines.", "B) Strive to take control of their narrative.", "C) Do their best to impress the interviewer.", "D) Repeat the information on their resume."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Repeat the information on their resume.\n【定位】But that's what most people will do, as it's their instinct to recite things that are already on\ntheir résumé.\n但这正是大多数人会做的，因为重复简历上已有的内容是他们的本能。\n【信号】主语 most people 的直接陈述。信号是题干主语 most people 在原文原样出现，紧跟其\n后的 recite things that are already on their résumé 就是这群人的实际动作，定位干脆。\n【替换】recite things that are already on their résumé ↔ Repeat the information on their resume\n同义词替换 recite=repeat\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Follow their career coaches' guidelines 盗用了原文 professional career coaches\n这一原词，但原文只说教练会\"准备/指导\"，从没说大多数人\"遵循教练指南\"，与题干 most\npeople 的实际动作无关。\n借⽤原⽂：professional career coaches prepare people for\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 take control of their narrative 直接搬原文原句 take control of the narrative，但那\n是说话人推荐\"应该\"做的，而题干问的是 most people \"实际\"做的，张冠李戴。\n借⽤原⽂：take control of the narrative\nC) 未提及\n选项 Do their best to impress the interviewer 在原文里找不到对应句，说话人没说大多\n数人会\"努力打动面试官\"，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "Why should job seekers talk with partners, friends and co-workers?", "options": ["A) To reflect on their past achievements as well as failures.", "B) To produce examples for different interview questions.", "C) To discuss important details they are going to present.", "D) To identify a broad general strength to elaborate on."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To produce examples for different interview questions.\n【定位】To come up with multiple career accomplishments or examples for different interview\nquestions, job seekers should talk with others, especially people who know them, partners,\nfriends or co-workers who will bring up different stories than the ones they remember.\n为了想出多种职业成就，或为不同面试问题准备例子，求职者应当与他人交谈，尤其是了解\n自己的人——伴侣、朋友或同事，他们会带来一些自己记不起的不同故事。\n【信号】目的状语 To… 在前点明意图。句首的目的状语 To come up with multiple career\naccomplishments or examples for different interview questions 直接交代了\"和他人\n聊\"的目的，是典型的目的信号，听到句首 To do 就知道后面动作的原因在这。\n【替换】examples for different interview questions ↔\nTo produce examples for different interview questions\n近原句改写 come up with=produce\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 To reflect on their past achievements as well as failures 借了原文 getting\nreflective about what made their career accomplishments 的\nreflective/accomplishments，但原文只谈\"成就\"，从未提 failures，且\"反思\"是讲怎么编\n故事，不是\"和朋友聊\"的目的。\n借⽤原⽂：getting reflective about what made their career accomplishments something they're\nproud of\nC) 未提及\n选项 To discuss important details they are going to present 在原文里没有对应：原文\n说和他人聊是为了\"想出例子\"，不是\"讨论要展示的重要细节\"，属无中生有。\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 To identify a broad general strength to elaborate on 直接搬原文 pick a broad\ngeneral strength to elaborate on，但原文前面有否定 shouldn't——这是叫人\"别\"挑笼统\n优点，被选项正话反说成\"目的\"。\n借⽤原⽂：Candidates shouldn't pick a broad general strength to elaborate on", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What other important preparations should job seekers make before an interview?", "options": ["A) Getting acquainted with the human resources personnel.", "B) Finding out why the company provides the job opening.", "C) Figuring out what benefits the company is able to offer them.", "D) Tailoring their expectations to the company's long-term goal."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Finding out why the company provides the job opening.\n【定位】Secondly, candidates should know what's at stake for the company with this job opening.\nWhat they really are asking you is \"Tell me why you are going to help me.\"\n第二，求职者应当弄清楚这个职位空缺对公司而言的利害所在。他们真正问你的是\"告诉我\n你将如何帮到我\"。\n【信号】顺序词 Secondly 引出第二要点。信号是顺序词 Secondly，它接着第三段的\"First\"列出第\n二项准备：know what's at stake for the company with this job opening，顺着顺序词\n就能锁定本题答案段。\n【替换】know what's at stake for the company with this job opening ↔\nFinding out why the company provides the job opening\n概括改写 + 同义词替换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Getting acquainted with the human resources personnel 在原文里没有任何对\n应，说话人没提\"结识人力资源人员\"，属凭空添加。\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Figuring out what benefits the company is able to offer them 说\"弄清公司能给你\n什么福利\"，但原文紧接着说 Tell me why you are going to help me，强调的是\"你能帮公\n司什么\"，方向恰好相反。\n借⽤原⽂：Tell me why you are going to help me\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Tailoring their expectations to the company's long-term goal 借了原文反复出现\n的 company，又把第五段的 tailored to their audience 里的 tailor 搬过来拼凑，但原文\n从未让人\"按公司长期目标调整期望\"，是张冠李戴的拼装。\n借⽤原⽂：keep their career story focused and tailored to their audience", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) vacancy\n名词\n【词性槽】experiencing ___ rates 动名词后需名词修饰 rates\n【依据】vacancy rates = 空房率，与'酒店 70-80%'是直配术语\nHotels in San Francisco are experiencing __(26)__ rates between 70 and 80 percent.\n与上文'美国人取消出行、Amtrak 取消率暴增 300%'呼应，旧金山酒店'空房率高达\n70%-80%' 是疫情冲击酒店业的常识画面，vacancy rate 正是酒店业的标准术语。\n【竞争词】A) credentials credentials rates 不成术语\ncredentials 指资格证书，与酒店业指标无关，也无 credentials rates 这一表达。\nD) disruptions disruptions rates 不符英语表达\n英语里没有 disruption rates 这一惯用术语，且语义与 70-80% 这类百分比不直接对应。\nG) predictions predictions rates 语义不对\n酒店不会'体验预测率'；G 的语义在 30 题与'技术专家的反向预言'更对得上。\nH) preference preference rates 不成搭配\npreference 表偏好，rates of preference 极少用；且 70-80% 不是'偏好率'的典型数值表达。\nO) wedge wedge rates 完全不通\nwedge 既无搭配支持也无逻辑联系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·experiencing ___ rat"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) scale\n名词\n【词性槽】on this ___ 介词 + 限定词后需名词\n【依据】on this scale 表'如此规模'是地道固定搭配\nhave never tried online learning on this __(27)__ 想说大学'从未在如此规模上尝试过网\n课'，on this scale = '以这样的规模'，与'疫情把全美大学全部赶上网'的语境严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】A) credentials credentials 多用复数，且语义无关\ncredentials 指资历/证书，与'尝试网课的规模'无关，也不与 on this 搭配。\nD) disruptions 复数与 this 不一致\ndisruptions 是复数名词，与单数限定词 this 不一致（要复数得用 these），且 29 题需要动词\n宾语，这里更可能优先归名词后留作他用——但首要排除理由就是数不一致。\nG) predictions 复数且语义不符\npredictions 复数与 this 数不一致；语义上也讲不通'在这种预测上尝试网课'。\nH) preference on this preference 不构成英语习惯表达\npreference 通常与 for 搭配，on this preference 在'尝试网课'语境里既无搭配支持也无语义\n支撑。\nN) vacancy vacancy 与'网课规模'无关\nvacancy 指空缺/空房率，话题指向酒店或职位，放进'大学网课'语境完全跑题。\nO) wedge wedge 含义偏物理'楔子'，与抽象规模无关\nwedge 表示楔形物或楔入，与'尝试网课'毫无逻辑联系，且 on this wedge 不成固定搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·on this ___ 介词 + 限定词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) foreseeable\n形容词\n【词性槽】the ___ future 中需修饰名词 future 的形容词\n【依据】固定搭配 the foreseeable future（可预见的未来）\nasking employees to work from home for the __(28)__ future 表达'让员工在可预见的未\n来内居家办公'，foreseeable future 是英语里高度凝固的固定搭配，意思与语境完全吻合。\n【竞争词】C) cumulative cumulative future 不成搭配\ncumulative 修饰可累加的对象（效应、分数、影响），不与 future 连用，语义也对不上'可预见\n的未来'。\nK) subtle subtle 与'未来'语义不搭\nsubtle 强调'细微难察'，并不修饰时间段；the subtle future 既不是固定表达，也与上下文'居家\n办公的一段时期'无逻辑联系。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the ___ future 中需修饰名"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) strangle\n动词\n【词性槽】take ... , ___ ... and destroy ... 三并列谓语动词原形\n【依据】strangle economies = 扼杀经济，与 take lives/destroy routines 同向递进\nThe pandemic(大流行病) will take lives, __(29)__ economies and destroy routines 三个\n并列动作都讲'疫情造成的破坏'，strangle economies '扼杀经济' 正好与 take lives '夺走生\n命'、destroy routines '摧毁日常' 形成同向语义集，搭配也是英语新闻里常见说法。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·take ... , ___ ... a"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) predictions\n名词\n【词性槽】technologists' ___ to the contrary 所有格后需名词\n【依据】predictions to the contrary = 与之相反的（技术专家的）预言\nNo decentralizing technology so far—not telephones, not television, and not the\ninternet—has dented that human desire to shake hands, despite technologists'\n__(30)__ to the contrary. 意为：尽管技术专家有相反的预言，但分散化技术从未削弱过人\n们当面握手的欲望。predictions to the contrary 是英语里固定表达'反向预测'，且\ndespite 引导让步，正好对应'专家说会怎样，事实却没怎样'。\n【竞争词】A) credentials credentials 与'相反'逻辑无关\n技术专家的资历不会构成'相反'的让步语境，credentials to the contrary 既不成搭配也不成\n逻辑。\nD) disruptions disruptions 留作 31 题更贴\n31 题 'Small ___ create small societal shifts; big ones change things for good.' 这一对比正\n是为 disruptions 量身打造；放到 30 题则'相反的扰动'语义混乱。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·technologists' ___ t"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) disruptions\n名词\n【词性槽】Small ___ create ... 形容词后作主语的名词\n【依据】Small disruptions ... big ones 与下文'纽约交通大罢工、西班牙流感'同义呼应\nSmall __(31)__ create small societal shifts; big ones change things for good. 后紧跟\nThe New York transit strike of 1980 is __(32)__ with prompting several long-term\nchanges in the city 与 The Spanish flu pandemic of 1918 prompted the development\nof national health care in Europe.，举的全是大规模'扰动/中断'引发长期变革的例子。\ndisruptions（扰动、中断）正是这两件事的统称。\n【竞争词】A) credentials credentials 不会带来'社会变迁'\ncredentials 指资历/证书，与'引发社会变化'的因果链毫无关系，且与下文罢工、瘟疫等大事件\n无类属关系。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 A) —— credentials 词性是复数名词，初看也能填。Small __(31)__\ncreate small societal shifts 这一空，credentials 复数名词、词性完全符合，且'小资历带\n来小变化'读起来似有逻辑感；但回到后两句 The New York transit strike of 1980 和 The\nSpanish flu pandemic of 1918 提供的具体例子，credentials 与罢工、瘟疫毫无类属关\n系，只有 disruptions（扰动）能涵盖。防坑：抽象主语的空必须靠后文例证反推。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·Small ___ create ..."]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) credited\n动词\n【词性槽】is ___ with 系动词后过去分词作被动\n【依据】be credited with = 被归功于，与'罢工促成长期改变'契合\nThe New York transit strike of 1980 is __(32)__ with prompting several long-term\nchanges in the city 表'1980 年纽约交通大罢工被认为/被归功于推动了诸多长期改变'，be\ncredited with doing sth. 正是英语固定搭配。\n【竞争词】L) summoned be summoned with 不成搭配\nsummon 表'传唤、召集'，常用 be summoned to do/to a place，与 with 几乎不搭；语义\n上'被传唤推动改变'也读不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— summoned 词性、形态、过去分词样貌全对。The New\nYork transit strike of 1980 is __(32)__ with prompting several long-term changes in\nthe city 这一空，summoned 词性是过去分词，与 credited 极易混淆；考生若不熟 be\ncredited with 这一搭配，可能误以为'罢工被召唤来推动改变'也说得通。防坑：be V-\ned with 一定要回到固定搭配表里筛——summon 的常见搭配是 be summoned to，不\n是 with。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·is ___ with 系动词后过去分词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) preference\n名词\n【词性槽】a question of ___ 介词 of 后需名词\n【依据】a question of preference 表'并非偏好问题'\nHere and now, this might not even be a question of __(33)__. 上下文紧接 It's not clear\nthat the cruise industry will __(34)__.，作者想说在疫情现实面前，这已经不是'你想不想\n去、爱不爱去'的偏好选择问题，而是'有些行业能不能活下来'的生死问题。a question of\npreference 正是这层意思。\n【竞争词】A) credentials credentials 与'要不要做选择'语义不搭\ncredentials 指资历，把 a question of credentials 读出'这是资历问题'，与'邮轮业是否撑得\n住、公共交通是否破产'的现实拷问无关。\nD) disruptions 复数且 D 已留给 31 题语义更贴的位置\na question of disruptions 既不是常用搭配，且 disruptions 复数搭 a question of 也不自然；\nD 的最佳归宿在 31 题'小扰动/大扰动'的对照里。\nG) predictions G 已被 30 题'技术专家相反预言'锁定语义\npredictions 与第 30 题 technologists' __(30)__ to the contrary 高度契合；放到 33 题语义'这\n是预言问题'反而模糊。\nO) wedge wedge 不与 a question of 搭配\nwedge 表楔子/分歧楔，a question of wedge 既无搭配也无逻辑。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a question of ___ 介词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) survive\n动词\n【词性槽】will ___ 助动词后需动词原形\n【依据】survive 不及物用法贴合'能否存活'\nIt's not clear that the cruise industry will __(34)__. 想说'尚不清楚邮轮业是否能存活下\n来'，survive 可不带宾语，作不及物动词正好表'活下去/挺过去'，与 33 题确认的'已不是偏\n好问题，而是生死问题'承接顺畅。\n【竞争词】J) strangle strangle 是及物动词，且语义为'扼杀他者'\nstrangle 通常带宾语（strangle sb./sth.），用在句末 will strangle 既缺宾语，也讲不通'邮轮业\n将扼杀（谁？）'；J 留给 29 题作'扼杀经济'的及物用法。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·will ___ 助动词后需动词原形"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) credentials", "B) credited", "C) cumulative", "D) disruptions", "E) federal", "F) foreseeable", "G) predictions", "H) preference", "I) scale", "J) strangle", "K) subtle", "L) summoned", "M) survive", "N) vacancy", "O) wedge"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) federal\n形容词\n【词性槽】___ assistance 名词前需形容词作定语\n【依据】federal assistance 表'联邦援助'是政经语境固定表达\npublic transit won't go broke without __(35)__ assistance 谈的是公共交通若没有外部救\n助就会破产，结合美国语境，'联邦援助'最贴。federal assistance 也是英语新闻里常见的固\n定表达。\n【竞争词】C) cumulative cumulative 强调'累积',与'援助来源'不对应\ncumulative assistance（累积援助）不符合'救公共交通免于破产'这一关于资金来源/性质的表\n达，逻辑指向错。\nK) subtle subtle assistance 语义反向\nsubtle assistance 意指'细微/不易察觉的援助'，反而削弱救助力度，与'否则就会破产'要求的大\n额外部支持相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·___ assistance 名词前需形"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It seems some people today dream that a cutting-edge new technology might save them from the present ecological disaster.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「a cutting-edge new technology might save them」\n【定位】They suggest that geoengineering, cold fusion or faster-than-light spaceships might transcend\nonce and for all the terrestrial constraints of rising temperatures, lack of energy, scarcity of\nfood, lack of space, mountains of waste, polluted water—you name it.\n这些叙述暗示，地球工程、冷聚变或超光速飞船也许能一举超越地球的种种约束——升温、\n能源不足、食物匮乏、空间不足、堆积如山的垃圾、污染的水，凡此种种。\n【改写】a cutting-edge new technology might save them ↔\ngeoengineering, cold fusion or faster-than-light spaceships might transcend once and for all\nthe terrestrial constraints\n具体技术 → 上位类「尖端新技术」\nthe present ecological disaster ↔\nthe terrestrial constraints of rising temperatures, lack of energy, scarcity of food, lack of space,\nmountains of waste, polluted water\n多项生态约束 → 上位概括「当前生态灾难」\nsome people today dream ↔ Some of today's narratives about the future seem to suggest\n「叙述暗示」= 「人们梦想」\n【辨邻】 F F 段紧接 E 段，确实讨论「靠工程师/技术解题」，但 if we envisage our salvation\nto come from a deus ex machina…we might be looking in the wrong place 是反\n对态度；而陈述句 It seems some people today dream 只描述「有人这样想」，并\n未带否定判断，所以源头段是描述「梦想」本身的 E，而不是反驳它的 F。\nif we envisage our salvation to come from a deus ex machina, from a divine engineer or a tech\nsolutionist who will miraculously conjure up a new source of energy or another cure-all with\nrevolutionary potency, we might be looking in the wrong place", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "According to one great thinker, it is most unfortunate if we lose the ability to think differently.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「one great thinker」+「lose the ability to think differently」\n【定位】Ernst Bloch, one of the leading philosophers of the future, wrote that 'the most tragic form of\nloss…is the loss of the capacity to imagine that things could be different'.\n恩斯特·布洛赫——未来研究的领军哲学家之一——曾写道：「最悲剧性的失落……是失去想\n象事物可以不同的能力。」\n【改写】one great thinker ↔ Ernst Bloch, one of the leading philosophers of the future\n具名哲学家 = 「一位伟大思想家」\nit is most unfortunate if we lose the ability to think differently ↔\nthe most tragic form of loss…is the loss of the capacity to imagine that things could be\ndifferent\n「最悲剧的失落」= 「最不幸的事」+名动转换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Urgent attention should be paid to the ecological problems we have created in our pursuit of a comfortable life.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「pursuit of a comfortable life」造成生态问题\n【定位】Today we can no longer ignore the ecological curses that we have released in our search for\nwarmth and comfort.\n今天我们再也不能忽视在追求温暖与舒适过程中所释放的那些生态诅咒。\n【改写】Urgent attention should be paid to the ecological problems ↔\nwe can no longer ignore the ecological curses\n「不再忽视」= 「必须紧急关注」\nwe have created in our pursuit of a comfortable life ↔\nwe have released in our search for warmth and comfort\n「追求舒适」= 「追求温暖与舒适」\n【辨邻】 D D 段虽也讲生态问题、且 Acceleration is the signature of our time，但只在讨论\n「加速」的曲线，没有把生态问题归因于「追求舒适生活」，也没有「应紧急关注」的\n呼吁意味，缺了陈述句的关键要素。\nSpecies extinction, deforestation, damming of rivers, occurrence of floods, the depletion of ozone,\nthe degradation of ocean systems and many other areas are all experiencing acceleration", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Even in the fast-food nation America, the number of vegetarians is on the rise.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「fast-food nation America」+「vegetarians is on the rise」\n【定位】In 2017, 6 per cent of US consumers claimed to be strictly vegetarian, up from 1 per cent\nin 2014.\n2017 年，6% 的美国消费者自称是严格的素食主义者，比 2014 年的 1% 有所上升。\n【改写】Even in the fast-food nation America ↔ Even in the United States—the fast-food nation\n「fast-food nation America」≈ 原文同名表述\nthe number of vegetarians is on the rise ↔\n6 per cent of US consumers claimed to be strictly vegetarian, up from 1 per cent in 2014\n数据上升 = 「数量上升」\n【辨邻】 J J 段确实讲 Slow Food movement 起源与扩张，但聚焦 in Italy in the 1980s 与\nmore than 160 countries 的概览，并没有具体到美国素食者比例上升，因此不是本题\n源头。\nUnder the leadership of Carlo Petrini, the Slow Food movement began in Piedmont", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The deterioration of the ecological system is accelerating because of the dramatic increase of human production and consumption.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「dramatic increase of human production and consumption」导致生态恶化加速\n【定位】The acceleration of human production, consumption and travel has changed the animate and\ninanimate spheres. It has echoed through natural processes on which humans depend. Species\nextinction, deforestation, damming of rivers, occurrence of floods, the depletion of ozone, the\ndegradation of ocean systems and many other areas are all experiencing acceleration.\n人类生产、消费与出行的加速已经改变了生物与非生物的世界，并在我们依赖的自然过程中\n产生回响。物种灭绝、毁林、河流筑坝、洪水频发、臭氧消耗、海洋系统退化等众多领域都\n在经历加速。\n【改写】The deterioration of the ecological system is accelerating ↔\nSpecies extinction, deforestation, damming of rivers, occurrence of floods, the depletion of\nozone, the degradation of ocean systems and many other areas are all experiencing\nacceleration\n生态领域清单的加速 = 「生态系统恶化加速」\nbecause of the dramatic increase of human production and consumption ↔\nThe acceleration of human production, consumption and travel has changed the animate and\ninanimate spheres. It has echoed through natural processes on which humans depend\n「人类生产消费加速影响自然」= 「生产消费剧增导致生态恶化」\n【辨邻】 C C 段虽然也说 environmental threats…are multiplying at a dizzying rate，但归因\n模糊（只笼统讲「搜寻温暖与舒适」），并未明确指向「生产消费的剧增」这一陈述句\n的关键原因；本题严格要求因果两端对齐，故排除。\nYear after year, faster and faster, consumption outpaces the biological capacity of our planet", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "It is obvious that solutions must be found to curb the fast worsening environment and social acceleration.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「solutions must be found to curb」环境与社会加速\n【定位】This much is clear: we need to find ways that help us flatten the hockey-stick curves that\nreflect our ever-faster pace of ecological destruction and social acceleration.\n这一点是清楚的：我们需要找到办法，把那条反映生态破坏与社会加速越来越快的「曲棍球\n棒」曲线压平。\n【改写】It is obvious that solutions must be found ↔ This much is clear: we need to find ways\n「显然要找方法」= 「we need to find ways」\nto curb the fast worsening environment and social acceleration ↔\nflatten the hockey-stick curves that reflect our ever-faster pace of ecological destruction and\nsocial acceleration\n「压平曲线」= 「遏制」+ 术语原样复现\n【辨邻】 F F 段以问句 So what do we do? 结尾，意在引出后文，但没有出现「social\nacceleration」这一关键术语，也没有「This much is clear: we need to find ways」\n式的明确主张，因此不是本题源头。\nGlobal heating and environmental degradation are not technological problems. They are highly\npolitical issues that are informed by powerful interests", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Many people believe changing the world is possible, though it may take time and involve setbacks.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「changing the world is possible」+「take time and involve setbacks」\n【定位】Our world is full of—mostly untold—stories of slow hope, driven by the idea that change\nis possible. They are 'slow' in their unfolding, and they are slow because they come\nwith setbacks.\n我们的世界充满了大多未被讲述的「慢希望」故事，它们由「改变是可能的」这一信念所驱\n动。它们的展开是缓慢的，因为它们伴随挫折。\n【改写】Many people believe changing the world is possible ↔ driven by the idea that change is possible\n「人们相信」= 「被这一信念驱动」\nit may take time and involve setbacks ↔\nThey are 'slow' in their unfolding, and they are slow because they come with setbacks\n「展开缓慢 + 伴随挫折」= 「需要时间 + 伴随挫折」\n【辨邻】 H H 段也提到 slow hope，但它的命题是「我们需要多重故事而非英雄叙事」，强调对\n策方向；并未直接表述「人们相信改变可能且过程会有挫折」的总观点，那是 A 段的\n开篇定调。\nwe need stories, not only of what Rob Nixon of Princeton University has called the 'slow violence'\nof environmental degradation…but also stories of what I call 'slow hope'", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "It might be wrong to expect that our world would be saved at one stroke with some miraculous technology.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「saved at one stroke with some miraculous technology」是错的\n【定位】The fact that we now imagine our planet as a whole does not mean that the 'rescue' of our\nplanet will come with one big global stroke of genius and technology. It will more likely\ncome by many small acts.\n我们现在能整体地想象这个星球，这并不意味着对它的「拯救」会来自一次全球性的天才与\n技术的妙笔；它更可能由许多小行动促成。\n【改写】It might be wrong to expect that our world would be saved at one stroke with some miraculous\ntechnology\n↔\nwe envisage our salvation to come from a deus ex machina, from a divine engineer or a tech\nsolutionist who will miraculously conjure up a new source of energy or another cure-all with\nrevolutionary potency, we might be looking in the wrong place\n「找错地方」= 「这种期待可能是错的」\nour world would be saved at one stroke ↔\nthe 'rescue' of our planet will come with one big global stroke of genius and technology\n「一举」表述原样复现\n【辨邻】 E E 段确实提到 we too, like Prometheus, will be saved by a new Hercules, a divine\nengineer 这一技术救世幻想，但只是描述这种说法，没有判断「这是错的」；而陈述\n句 It might be wrong to expect 带明确批判态度，应去 F 段。\nSome of today's narratives about the future seem to suggest that we too, like Prometheus, will be\nsaved by a new Hercules, a divine engineer", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "It is human nature to cherish hopes for a better world.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「human nature to cherish hopes for a better world」\n【定位】What makes us 'authentic' as humans are visions of our 'potential'. In other words: living in\nhope makes us human.\n让我们作为人类「本真」的，是对自身「潜能」的种种愿景。换句话说：活在希望中让我们\n成为人。\n【改写】It is human nature to cherish hopes ↔ living in hope makes us human\n「A让我们成为人」= 「A是人性」\nfor a better world ↔ the outlines of a better world\n「更好世界」原词复现\n【辨邻】 A A 段确实提到 driven by the idea that change is possible，但只把「改变可能」当\n作驱动故事的信念，并未把希望或追求更好世界定义为「人性」；本题要求的是「人性\n= 怀抱希望」这一定义性命题，故而去 I 段。\nOur world is full of—mostly untold—stories of slow hope, driven by the idea that change\nis possible", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Technology has given us humans the power to change the natural world, but we have paid a price for the change.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】「Technology」改变自然 +「paid a price」\n【定位】Just as in the myth, technology—first fire and stone tools, and later farming, the steam engine\nand industry, fossil fuels, chemicals and nuclear power—has allowed us to alter and control\nthe natural world. The myth also reminds us that these advances have come at a price: as a\npunishment for Prometheus' crime, the gods created Pandora, and they gave her a box filled\nwith evils and curses.\n正如神话所讲，技术——最初是火与石器，后来是农业、蒸汽机和工业、化石燃料、化学品\n和核能——让我们得以改造并控制自然界。神话也提醒我们，这些进步是付出代价的：作为\n对普罗米修斯之罪的惩罚，众神创造了潘多拉，并给了她一个装满邪恶与诅咒的盒子。\n【改写】Technology has given us humans the power to change the natural world ↔\ntechnology—first fire and stone tools, and later farming, the steam engine and industry, fossil\nfuels, chemicals and nuclear power—has allowed us to alter and control the natural world\n「允许我们改造」= 「给了我们改变的力量」\nwe have paid a price for the change ↔ these advances have come at a price\n「付出代价」原样复现\n【辨邻】 C C 段确实在讲 the ecological curses that we have released，但它承袭的是 B 段提\n出的「技术付代价」命题、展开其后果；陈述句要求的「技术赋予改造自然之力 + 已\n付代价」总命题首次完整出现在 B 段，故 C 段不是源头。\nIn engineering and exploiting and transforming our habitat, we have opened tens of thousands of\nPandora's boxes\nPARAGRAPH MAP · 段落主旨图\n个段落各讲了什么\n逐段一句主旨 + 该段最具区分度的「信息指纹」，是逐句回扫定位的工作台。\nA 开宗明义：世界上充满了大量未被讲述的「慢希望」故事，它们由「改变是可能的」\n这一信念驱动，过程缓慢且伴随挫折。\n信息指纹 「慢希望」总纲\nB 用普罗米修斯盗火的神话作类比：从火、石器到农业、蒸汽机、化石燃料、化学品、\n核能，技术让人类改造并控制了自然，但代价是潘多拉魔盒被打开，疾病与灾难随之\n释放。\n信息指纹 普罗米修斯神话 + 技术清单 + 潘多拉代价\nC 当今人类不能再忽视为追求温暖与舒适而释放出的生态诅咒——环境威胁已从区域扩\n展到全球，且以惊人的速度成倍增加，我们正在用尽时间。\n信息指纹 为舒适付出的生态代价 + 全球化威胁加速\nD 加速是当今时代的标志：人口、经济、消费长期几乎不增长，到 19 世纪中、尤其 20\n世纪中以后骤升，物种灭绝、毁林、筑坝、洪水、臭氧消耗、海洋退化等都呈「曲棍\n球棒」式跃升。\n信息指纹 「曲棍球棒」加速曲线 + 数字佐证\nE\n今天有些关于未来的叙述暗示我们将再次被某位「神工」式的工程师拯救，靠地球工\n程、冷聚变或超光速飞船一举超越升温、能源短缺、食物匮乏、太空、垃圾、污水等\n地球性约束。\n信息指纹 新「神工」迷思 + 尖端技术清单\nF 然而，把救赎寄托在「解围之神」式的工程师或技术解决主义者身上，可能是找错了\n地方——地球的拯救更可能由许多小行动促成，而非一次全球性的天才妙笔；任何重\n大变革都会带来意料之外的后果。\n信息指纹 反「一举拯救」论 + 「many small acts」\nG\n我们必须找到办法把反映生态破坏与社会加速的「曲棍球棒」曲线压平；承认人类操\n纵地球带来的破坏性，也意味着可以想象人类的努力能在未来几个世纪里建一个破坏\n更少的世界。\n信息指纹 「压平曲线」 + 必须找解决之道\nH 为对抗灾难恐惧，我们需要的不是一个大叙事或英雄式拯救，而是许多悄悄走向更有\n希望未来的故事——既要讲 Rob Nixon 所说的「慢暴力」，也要讲作者所说的「慢\n希望」。\n信息指纹 Rob Nixon「慢暴力」 + 多重叙事\nI 我们既要承认生态困境，也需要积极变革的语言；引用恩斯特·布洛赫《希望的原\n理》：最悲剧的失落是失去想象事物可以不同的能力——希望源于恐惧与危机，是与\n「乌托邦愿景」并行的创造力；活在希望中让我们成为人。\n信息指纹 Ernst Bloch + 《希望的原理》 + 「失去想象」\nJ 草根运动可以让改变扩散到原发地之外——以 1980 年代起于意大利皮埃蒙特的「慢\n食」运动为例：Carlo Petrini 领导下，它对抗战后廉价工业化食物，如今已在 160 多\n个国家开展数千个项目。\n信息指纹 Slow Food 运动 + Carlo Petrini + 起源地与扩张\nK\n慢食无法逆转全球食物经济，但能让本地饮食传统与环境繁荣；即便在「快餐之国」\n美国，小农场与城市菜园也在兴起，2017 年严格素食者占比已从 2014 年的 1% 升至\n6%；正如 Wendell Berry 所言，「吃即农业行为」，慢希望由此推进。\n信息指纹 美国素食比例上升 + Wendell Berry + Urban Agriculture Toolkit", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is more visible in India than anywhere else according to the passage?", "options": ["A) People's positive views on the proposals for a \"planetary diet\".", "B) People's reluctance to be compelled to eat plant-based food.", "C) People's preferences for the kind of food they consume.", "D) People's unwillingness to give up their eating habits."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is more visible in India than anywhere else 用 what 直接问一个被作者点名的事\n实，无 infer、imply、main idea 这类信号词。\n【拆句】Q46 主干是 What is more visible in India；than anywhere else 是比较状语，强调印度\n比别处更明显。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Vegetarians would prefer not to be compelled to eat meat. Yet the reverse compulsion is\nhidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet.\" Nowhere is this more visible\nthan in India.\n素食者不愿被强迫吃肉。然而，相反方向的强迫却藏在新的植物基『星球饮食』提案里。这\n种反向强迫在印度比在任何地方都更明显。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\npositive views on the proposals 把原文对该提案的批评立场反转为积极看法，与全文\n及定位句中 hidden、aggravate 等贬义语义完全相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe reverse compulsion is hidden in the proposals for a new plant-based \"planetary diet\" ↔\nPeople's reluctance to be compelled to eat plant-based food\nNowhere is this more visible than in India ↔\nPeople's reluctance to be compelled to eat plant-based food\n预判得到的反向强迫在印度最明显，等于 People's reluctance to be compelled to eat\nplant-based food，完全对齐。\nC) 答非所问\npreferences for the kind of food they consume 说的是对所吃食物种类的偏好，是泛\n泛的口味话题；原文要点是被强迫，不是偏好，没有回答 more visible in India 的具体\n所指。\nD) 张冠李戴\nunwillingness to give up their eating habits 谈的是不愿放弃饮食习惯，原文 P1 只谈\n被反向强迫，并未在此处讨论放弃既有习惯；P5 虽出现 give up traditional foods，但\n与题干 more visible in India 这一焦点错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What would the EAT-Lancet Commission's report do to many people in countries like India?", "options": ["A) Radically change their dietary habits.", "B) Keep them further away from politics.", "C) Make them even more undernourished.", "D) Substantially reduce their food choices."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What would the EAT-Lancet Commission's report do to many people 用 what…do 直\n问报告对人群产生的具体影响，无推断信号词。\n【拆句】Q47 What would…do to many people 是询问主语对宾语造成的影响；would 表预测语\n气，是基于原文给出的虚拟后果，不是推断信号。\n【定位】In countries like India, that call could become a tool to aggravate an already tense political\nsituation and stress already undernourished populations.\n在像印度这样的国家，那种呼吁可能变成加剧本已紧张的政治局势、并使本已营养不良的人\n群处境更糟的工具。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nRadically change their dietary habits 谈彻底改变饮食习惯，定位句 P2 只讲加剧政治\n紧张和加重营养不良，并未提报告会让人群彻底改变饮食习惯。\nB) 与原文相反\nKeep them further away from politics 说让他们远离政治，但原文 aggravate an\nalready tense political situation 是加剧政治紧张，与让人远离政治方向相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nstress already undernourished populations ↔ Make them even more undernourished\n预判得到的让营养不良人群更糟，等于 Make them even more undernourished，完\n全对齐。\nD) 偷换概念\nSubstantially reduce their food choices 谈大幅减少食物选项。原文 substantially\nreducing consumption of animal source foods 中 substantially reducing 修饰的是\n动物源食物的消费量，不是食物选项的数量；选项把减少消费偷换成减少选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about food consumption in India?", "options": ["A) People's diet will not change due to the EAT-Lancet report.", "B) Many people simply do not have access to foods they prefer.", "C) There is a growing popularity of a cereal-heavy vegetarian diet.", "D) Policymakers help remove the barriers to people's choice of food."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about food consumption in India 问的是文章关\n于某话题告诉了我们什么事实，没有 infer 或 imply。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What do we learn from the passage；about food consumption in India 是\n话题限定语，结构清晰、无陷阱。\n【定位】In India, however, there is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and\nwhat they have to consume because of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture,\ncost, geography, etc.\n然而在印度，人们想要消费的与不得不消费的之间存在巨大差异，原因是阶级、宗教、文\n化、成本、地理等数不清的障碍。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nPeople's diet will not change due to the EAT-Lancet report 谈饮食不会因 EAT 报告\n而改变。原文 P4 谈的是印度人想吃和能吃的差距，并未对是否会因报告改变作出预\n言；P2 反而暗示该报告会带来负面影响。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthere is a vast difference between what people would wish to consume and what they have to\nconsume\n↔ Many people simply do not have access to foods they prefer\nbecause of innumerable barriers around class, religion, culture, cost, geography, etc ↔\nMany people simply do not have access to foods they prefer\n预判得到的想吃的不等于能吃的，等于 do not have access to foods they prefer，完\n全对齐。\nC) 偷换概念\ngrowing popularity of a cereal-heavy vegetarian diet 谈以谷物为主的素食日益流\n行。原文 Policymakers in India have traditionally pushed for a cereal-heavy 说的\n是政策制定者『推行』，是自上而下，不是日益流行；growing popularity 把行为主体\n从政策制定者偷换为民众自发追捧。\nD) 与原文相反\nPolicymakers help remove the barriers to people's choice of food 谈决策者帮人民\n扫除饮食选择的障碍。原文 P4 的决策者是在吃肉人群上强推素食——是在制造或加固\n障碍，而非帮人扫除；方向完全反转。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the passage say about a plant-based diet modeled on India?", "options": ["A) It may benefit populations whose traditional diet is meat-based.", "B) It may be another blow to the economy in developing countries.", "C) It may help narrow the gap between the rich and poor countries.", "D) It may worsen the nourishment problem in low-income countries."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about a plant-based diet modeled on India 用 say about\n问文章对某概念明说了什么，按细节题处理。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What does the passage say；about a plant-based diet modeled on India\n是话题限定语，过去分词短语 modeled on India 后置修饰 diet。无陷阱。\n【定位】Which is why calls for a plant-based diet modeled on India risk offering another whip with\nwhich to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries.\n这正是为什么以印度为模板的植物基饮食呼声有可能成为又一根抽打发展中国家本已脆弱社\n群的鞭子。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nbenefit populations whose traditional diet is meat-based 说惠及以肉食为主的人\n群，原文 P8 和 P9 明确这是对弱势人群的伤害，方向相反；六级常借 benefit 这种正向\n动词制造极性反转。\nB) 偷换概念\nanother blow to the economy in developing countries 把打击对象从弱势社群（营\n养、民生）偷换成经济。原文 P8 beat 的是 vulnerable communities，不是\neconomy；P11 末尾的 economic cost 也不是本题定位段的论点。\nC) 无中生有\nnarrow the gap between the rich and poor countries 谈缩小贫富国差距，原文\nP8、P9 全程批评该饮食对发展中国家的伤害，从未谈缩小贫富差距，是空降话题。\nD) ✓ 正确\nanother whip with which to beat already vulnerable communities in developing countries ↔\nworsen the nourishment problem in low-income countries\nundernourished children are known to benefit from the consumption of milk and other animal\nsource foods\n↔ worsen the nourishment problem in low-income countries\n预判得到的加重弱势国家营养不良，等于 worsen the nourishment problem in low-\nincome countries，完全对齐。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "How does the Indian government respond to the EAT-Lancet Commission's proposals?", "options": ["A) It accepts them at the expense of the long-term interests of its people.", "B) It intends them to spark conversations among all Indian stakeholders.", "C) It gives them approval regardless of opposition from nutrition experts.", "D) It welcomes them as a tool to address chronic hunger and malnutrition."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How does the Indian government respond to the EAT-Lancet Commission's\nproposals 问政府如何回应，回应在文中有明示，是细节题而非主观态度题。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 How does the Indian government respond；to the EAT-Lancet\nCommission's proposals 是 respond 的介宾搭配。结构平铺直叙。\n【定位】The government, however, seems to have given the report a thumbs-up. Rather than\naddressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to wholesome and\nnutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-dependent solutions,\nignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local food systems. It's a\nmodel full of danger for future generations.\n然而，政府似乎对该报告点了赞。它不去通过改善人们获取健康、营养密度高的食物来解决\n慢性饥饿和营养不良，反而为公司依赖型方案敞开大门，忽视环境和经济代价，而这会摧毁\n本地食物体系。这是一个对未来世代充满危险的模式。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ngiven the report a thumbs-up ↔ It accepts them\nRather than addressing chronic hunger and malnutrition through an improved access to\nwholesome and nutrient-dense foods, the government is opening the door for company-\ndependent solutions, ignoring the environmental and economic cost, which will destroy local\nfood systems\n↔ at the expense of the long-term interests of its people\n预判得到的接受但牺牲长远利益，等于 It accepts them at the expense of the long-\nterm interests of its people，完全对齐。\nB) 张冠李戴\nintends them to spark conversations 把 EAT-Lancet claimed its intention\nwas to 的主语（EAT 委员会）安到政府头上；spark conversations 的发出者不是\nIndian government。\nC) 无中生有\nregardless of opposition from nutrition experts 谈不顾营养专家的反对。原文 P10\n提到的是被排除在外的 vocal critics of the food processing industry and food\nfortification strategies 和 India's farmers，并未明确说营养专家反对，且更没有说政\n府是不顾这些反对而通过。\nD) 与原文相反\nwelcomes them as a tool to address chronic hunger and malnutrition 把报告说成\n解决饥饿与营养不良的工具，但原文 Rather than addressing chronic hunger and\nmalnutrition 明确指出政府正是没有用它来解决这些问题；方向反转。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is characteristic of a chronic complainer, according to psychiatrist Eric Berne?", "options": ["A) They only feel angry about their ill treatment and resent whoever tries to help.", "B) They are chronically unhappy and ceaselessly find fault with people around them.", "C) They constantly dismiss others' proposals while taking no responsibility for tackling the problem.", "D) They lack the knowledge and basic skills required for successful conversations with their interlocutors."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is characteristic of a chronic complainer, according to psychiatrist Eric Berne?\n直接问某人观点下的某对象的特征，答案应当在原文里有逐字或同义对应。\n【拆句】题干是一个 What 引导的特殊疑问句，主干 What is characteristic of a chronic\ncomplainer 中 characteristic 作表语形容词，意思是\"……所特有的\"；尾部 according to\npsychiatrist Eric Berne 是状语，限定\"按照谁的说法\"，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The reason, of course, is that on some level they don't want a solution; they want to be\nvalidated in their position that the world is out to get them. If they can \"win\" the game—\ndismissing every suggestion until their interlocutor gives up in annoyance—they get to feel\npleasurably righteous in their resentments and excused from any obligation to change.\n原因当然是，在某种程度上他们并不想要解决方案；他们想要的是自己\"世界与我作对\"的立\n场被认可。如果他们能\"赢\"——通过驳回每一个建议直到对话者厌烦放弃——他们就能在怨\n恨中感到正当的快感，并被免除任何改变的责任。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说对话者最终 gives up in annoyance（厌烦放弃），并未说抱怨者对帮忙的人\n怀有怨恨；resent whoever tries to help 是无根据的扩展。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文中的抱怨指向情境本身（their relationship, job, or other situation），不是\"挑周\n围人毛病\"；ceaselessly find fault with people around them 把抱怨对象从\"处境\"偷\n换成了\"周围人\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\ndismissing every suggestion ↔ constantly dismiss others' proposals\nexcused from any obligation to change ↔ taking no responsibility for tackling the problem\n预判要求同时含\"驳回建议\"与\"不愿担责\"两个语义；只有 They constantly dismiss\nothers' proposals while taking no responsibility for tackling the problem. 完整覆\n盖了这两个语义。\nD) 无中生有\n原文没有任何处提到抱怨者缺乏对话的知识或技能；他们驳回建议是不愿改变，不是不\n会对话。lack the knowledge and basic skills 在原文找不到出处。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does the author try to illustrate with the example of the newborn on one's doorstep?", "options": ["A) People tend to think that one should not be held responsible for others' mistakes.", "B) It is easy to become attached to the position of overlooking one's own fault.", "C) People are often at a loss when confronted with a number of choices.", "D) A distinction should be drawn between responsibility and fault."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （例证目的题）\n【判型】What does the author try to illustrate with the example of the newborn on one's\ndoorstep? 明确询问\"作者用某例子要说明什么\"，是典型例证目的题特征。\n【拆句】题干主干 What does the author try to illustrate，with 引出方式状语 with the example\nof the newborn on one's doorstep，介词 of 后接同位语说明例子内容；句子结构清晰，\n无从句陷阱。\n【定位】For example, if I were to discover a newborn at my front door, it wouldn't be my fault, but it\nmost certainly would be my responsibility.\n举例来说，如果我在门口发现一个新生儿，这不会是我的过错，但毫无疑问会是我的责任。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文例子说的是\"事情不是你的错\"（自己没造成），并非\"别人犯了错\"；not be held\nresponsible for others' mistakes 把 fault 错绑到\"他人\"身上。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是\"易倾向于以'非我之责'为由不作为\"，并非\"忽视自身过错\"；B 把 attached\nto the position 的内容偷换为 overlooking one's own fault。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文提到 There would be choices to make 只是顺带说明\"必须选择、无法回避\"，并\n不是说\"人面对多重选择会不知所措\"；C 抓住 choices 一个词放大成完全不同的论点。\nD) ✓ 正确\nit wouldn't be my fault, but it most certainly would be my responsibility ↔\nA distinction should be drawn between responsibility and fault.\nresponsibility/fault fallacy ↔ between responsibility and fault\n例子要说明的不是\"门口婴儿怎么处理\"，而是\"过错≠责任\"这一概念区分；只有 A\ndistinction should be drawn between responsibility and fault. 直接表达了这一\n区分。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What does the author advise people to do to chronic complainers?", "options": ["A) Stop them from going further by agreeing with them.", "B) Listen to their complaints ardently and sympathetically.", "C) Ask them to validate their beliefs with further evidence.", "D) Persuade them to clarify the confusion they have caused."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author advise people to do to chronic complainers? 问的是一个具体可\n操作的动作建议，答案在原文里必有直接出处。\n【拆句】题干 advise sb. to do sth. 的句型变体；advise people to do 是 What 提出的疑问中心，\nto chronic complainers 是介词短语作动作对象状语，结构直白。\n【定位】Should you find yourself on the receiving end of this kind of complaining, there's an\ningenious way to shut it down—which is to agree with it, ardently.\n如果你发现自己正承受这种抱怨，有一个巧妙的办法可以让它停下来——那就是热烈地表示\n同意。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nagree with it, ardently ↔ agreeing with them\nshut it down ↔ Stop them from going further\n预判要求同时含\"阻止抱怨\"和\"通过附和的方式\"两层语义；只有 Stop them from going\nfurther by agreeing with them. 同时覆盖了这两层。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文方法是\"赞同\"，不是\"倾听+同情\"；Listen to their complaints ardently and\nsympathetically 把 agree 偷换为 listen，把 ardently 这一描述\"赞同强度\"的副词错配\n到\"倾听\"上。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文的策略是替对方做 validation（you're confirming them），让他自己听腻；C 反\n过来要求对方自己 validate，方向与作者建议相反。\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文没有出现\"让抱怨者澄清他们造成的混乱\"这一建议；confusion 一词来自 P2 But\nthere's a confusion here，指作者自己点出的概念混淆，不是抱怨者制造的。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What happens when chronic complainers receive over-validation?", "options": ["A) They are motivated to find ingenious ways to persuade their interlocutor.", "B) They are prompted to come up with ideas for making possible changes.", "C) They are stimulated to make more complaints.", "D) They are encouraged to start arguing back."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What happens when chronic complainers receive over-validation? 问的是某条件下出\n现的具体结果，答案必有原文出处。\n【拆句】题干主句 What happens，when 引导时间状语从句 when chronic complainers receive\nover-validation，从句主谓宾完整；结构简单。\n【定位】But for another, as Gottlieb notes, people confronted with over-validation often hear their\ncomplaints afresh and start arguing back. The notion that they're utterly powerless suddenly\nseems unrealistic—not to mention rather annoying—so they're prompted instead to generate\nideas about how they might change things.\n另一方面，正如 Gottlieb 所指出的那样，被过度赞同的人往往会重新听到自己的抱怨并开\n始反驳。\"自己完全无能为力\"的念头突然变得不切实际——而且相当令人烦躁——于是他们\n反而被促使去想出可以怎样改变现状的办法。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文 ingenious way 是用来形容\"应对抱怨者的方法\"（Q53 答案的策略），是 listener\n的策略，不是抱怨者自己的动作；A 把 ingenious 嫁接到抱怨者身上，方向错位。\nB) ✓ 正确\nprompted instead to generate ideas about how they might change things ↔\nprompted to come up with ideas for making possible changes\nprompted instead ↔ prompted\n预判要求答案是\"想出改变现状的办法\"，They are prompted to come up with ideas\nfor making possible changes. 与原文 prompted to generate ideas 几乎逐对应——\n动词、宾语、目的状语全部对位。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰说被过度赞同后\"省下了进一步的抱怨\"（spared further moaning）；C 反其\n道而行，方向相反。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 start arguing back 确实出现，但它只是因果链的中间步骤；后接 so they're\nprompted… 才是最终结果。D 截取中间环节作答，属于答非所问/以偏概全。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How can one stop being a chronic complainer according to the author?", "options": ["A) Analysing the so-called responsibility/fault fallacy.", "B) Avoiding hazardous traps in everyday social life.", "C) Assuming responsibility to free oneself.", "D) Awaiting something magical to happen."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （主旨题）\n【判型】How can one stop being a chronic complainer according to the author? 问\"如何停止\n成为本文话题角色\"，覆盖全文方法论，是主旨升华型问法。\n【拆句】题干 How can one stop doing sth.，主干 How can one stop being a chronic\ncomplainer，according to the author 是评注性状语；句式直白。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nresponsibility/fault fallacy 是文中提出的一个概念框架，作者用它来\"诊断\"问题；停止\n抱怨的方法不是\"分析这个谬误本身\"，而是基于它去承担责任。A 把分析工具当成停止\n抱怨的办法，停在前期分析阶段、未触及方案。\nB) 无中生有\n原文从未出现 hazardous traps 这一表述；末段提到 prison/freeing 是比喻\"逃避责任\n的状态\"，并不要求\"避开陷阱\"。B 用一个貌似学术的措辞掩盖与原文的脱节。\nC) ✓ 正确\nassuming responsibility feels unpleasant, but ends up being freeing ↔\nAssuming responsibility to free oneself.\neven if the other person is 100% in the wrong, there's nothing to be gained, long-term, from\nusing this as a justification to evade responsibility\n↔ Assuming responsibility\n预判要求答案同时含\"承担责任\"和\"获得解脱\"两个语义；只有 Assuming responsibility\nto free oneself. 同时覆盖这两个语义，并与末段升华句构成精确等值。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 something magical might happen 是描述对方（抱怨者）可能出现的转变，而不\n是建议你被动等待；停止抱怨需要主动承担责任，不是 Awaiting。D 把主动方案换成被\n动等待，方向完全相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}]});
